window.QUIZZES_HAND={"s2scicd":[{"q":"What does workload identity federation remove from a CI pipeline?","options":["The need for a cloud account","A long-lived cloud access key in repository secrets","The need to run tests before deploying","The deployment step, which the cloud now runs"],"answer":1,"why":"The pipeline proves who it is with a short-lived signed token instead of holding a stored credential, so there is nothing to leak or rotate.","whyWrong":["You still need one.","","Federation changes the credential, not the pipeline stages.","Your pipeline still deploys; only the credential changed."]},{"q":"What proves which pipeline is running?","options":["The repository name in the request body","The source IP address of the runner making the request","An OIDC token minted by the CI platform for that job","A shared secret in an environment variable"],"answer":2,"why":"The CI platform mints a token per job; its iss, aud and sub claims describe exactly which repository, workflow and ref is running.","whyWrong":["Anything in a request body is attacker-controlled.","Hosted runners come from shared pools, so the address proves nothing.","","That is the stored secret this replaces."]},{"q":"Your cloud trust policy checks the issuer and the signature, and nothing else. What is the consequence?","options":["Tokens expire too quickly for a long-running build job to finish","Nothing, a valid signature from the CI platform is proof enough","The audience and the subject are checked automatically by the cloud provider itself","Any repository on that CI platform, including an attacker's, can assume your role"],"answer":3,"why":"The CI platform signs tokens for every repository it hosts. A valid signature proves origin, not entitlement, the subject condition is the actual boundary.","whyWrong":["Lifetime is a separate setting and has no bearing on who is trusted.","The signature says where the token came from, not who should be trusted.","Nothing is checked for you. Every condition you leave out goes unenforced.",""]},{"q":"A trust policy names the repository but not the ref. What becomes possible?","options":["Nothing at all, the repository is the security boundary here","Only the default branch can deploy, because no other ref is ever issued a token","The token cannot be verified without a ref claim to bind it","A pull request, including one from a fork, can obtain production credentials"],"answer":3,"why":"Workflows run for pull requests too, and their tokens carry the same repository. Without a ref condition, any of them satisfies the policy.","whyWrong":["Branches within one repository are not equally trusted.","Any ref in the repository satisfies the policy, not just the default.","Verification works fine; the policy is what is too loose.",""]},{"q":"Why should the subject condition be an exact match rather than a prefix?","options":["Exact matching is required by the OIDC core spec","Prefix matching is slower to evaluate at scale","A prefix condition breaks the token signature check","A prefix on repo:acme/app also matches repo:acme/app-evil"],"answer":3,"why":"Subject strings are structured, and a prefix test crosses the boundary between segments, which is precisely how a lookalike repository qualifies.","whyWrong":["OIDC has no opinion on cloud trust policies.","Performance is irrelevant here.","The signature is over the token, not the policy.",""]}],"eibroker":[{"q":"What is an identity broker?","options":["A hub that is a relying party to upstream IdPs and an identity provider to downstream apps","A password vault that holds end-user credentials on behalf of the applications behind it","A service that buys verified identity data and resells it to businesses that need to check it","A directory replication tool that keeps user records in step between two separate directories"],"answer":0,"why":"Each side integrates once with the broker instead of every app integrating with every IdP.","whyWrong":["","That is a credential manager.","Nothing is sold.","That is directory sync."]},{"q":"What is the integration arithmetic a broker improves?","options":["It removes integrations entirely","From n + m to n x m","It does not change the count","From n x m to n + m"],"answer":3,"why":"Without a broker every app connects to every IdP. With one, each connects only to the hub.","whyWrong":["Each side still integrates once.","That is the wrong direction.","It changes it substantially.",""]},{"q":"Two upstream providers each have a user whose sub is 12345. What happens if the broker keys accounts on the raw sub?","options":["Nothing (subs are globally unique)","The broker generates a new sub","Two unrelated people are merged into one account","The second login is rejected"],"answer":2,"why":"A subject identifier is only unique within its issuer, so the pair (issuer, sub) is the identity, never the value alone.","whyWrong":["They are unique per issuer only.","Not unless you write that code.","","Nothing detects the collision."]},{"q":"A customer running their own IdP asserts the email ceo@yourcompany.com. Why is that dangerous?","options":["It breaks the signature on the assertion","It is not, email claims are verified by the protocol","Email claims are always ignored","A broker that links accounts by email hands it to them"],"answer":3,"why":"An upstream can assert any value it likes. A claim is only trustworthy where the provider has proven authority over it, which is why domain ownership must be verified.","whyWrong":["The assertion is signed correctly by their own IdP.","No protocol verifies that the asserter owns the address.","Many implementations use them, which is the problem.",""]},{"q":"Which cost of brokering is structural rather than incidental?","options":["It prevents multi-factor authentication, because the broker cannot forward a second factor challenge","It requires substantially more storage, since every assertion has to be retained for the audit trail","Everything behind it stops working when it is down, and compromising it exposes every app and upstream at once","It only works with SAML, so an organization adopting OIDC has to run a second broker alongside it"],"answer":2,"why":"The broker inherits the availability requirements of every application behind it and concentrates the blast radius, the IdP-as-blast-radius argument, one level up.","whyWrong":["Consistent MFA policy is a reason to use one.","Storage is trivial.","","It is protocol-agnostic by design."]}],"sml6":[{"q":"Why is a single cutover from SAML to OIDC a bad plan?","options":["SAML cannot be turned off once the IdP has it configured for a domain","Every undocumented integration is discovered at once, usually out of hours","The two protocols cannot run against the same IdP at the same time","OIDC needs new hardware to handle the extra token traffic"],"answer":1,"why":"Run both, migrate per application, and remove the old integration as a separate later change so a rollback is a configuration flip.","whyWrong":["It switches off cleanly once nothing depends on it.","","They coexist without trouble, which is what makes a staged migration work.","No hardware is involved. It is a protocol change."]},{"q":"What identifier problem appears during migration?","options":["OIDC provides no stable identifier for the user, only claims","Both protocols hand you the email address and nothing else as the identifier","Identifiers are assigned by the application at the first login","The SAML NameID and the OIDC sub are different values for the same person"],"answer":3,"why":"If the application stored the NameID as its user key, the OIDC login arrives looking like an entirely new user.","whyWrong":["It has sub, which is stable for a given issuer and client.","Neither should key on email, and email changes when people change name.","They come from the IdP. The app just stores what it receives.",""]},{"q":"Why must accounts NOT be auto-linked by email during a migration?","options":["Email lookups are slow at scale, so matching on a subject identifier keeps the migration within its window","Email is mutable and asserted by whichever side is speaking, so anyone able to influence it can attach to an existing account","SAML does not carry email as a standard attribute, so the value would be missing for many users","Email claims are encrypted in most assertions, so the migration tooling cannot read them reliably"],"answer":1,"why":"Explicit links populated deliberately are the safe answer; an unknown identifier means no account rather than a guess.","whyWrong":["Speed is irrelevant.","","It usually does.","They are not."]},{"q":"Which design choice makes a migration take weeks rather than a year?","options":["Migrating every application on one weekend with a rollback plan","Standardizing on SAML everywhere before the move to OIDC begins","Applications keying on an internal account id, not the protocol identifier","Choosing the same IdP vendor the old system already ran on"],"answer":2,"why":"An internal key means the protocol identifier is a lookup input rather than the primary key, so changing protocols does not change every stored reference.","whyWrong":["A flag day concentrates every failure into one weekend instead of spreading them out.","That is the state you are migrating out of, not a step that shortens the work.","","Vendor continuity does nothing about identifiers stored inside each application."]},{"q":"How do you know an application's migration is complete?","options":["When logins measured by protocol reach zero for SAML","When the vendor confirms the migration is complete on their side","When the OIDC integration is configured and tested","When the SAML integration has been deleted outright"],"answer":0,"why":"Instrument the login path by protocol first, then remove the old integration after a quiet period, the traffic you cannot see is what pages you.","whyWrong":["","The vendor cannot see your login traffic.","Configuration is the start of the migration, not the end.","Deleting first is how you discover the nightly job that still used it."]}],"pki7":[{"q":"What does certificate pinning change about trust?","options":["It encrypts the connection a second time, layering a private key over the negotiated session","Instead of accepting any certificate from any trusted CA, the client requires a specific pre-configured key","It extends certificate lifetime, since a pinned certificate no longer depends on the CA expiry","It removes the need for TLS, since the pin itself authenticates the peer before any handshake"],"answer":1,"why":"Any of the CAs in a trust store can issue for your domain; pinning narrows acceptance to a key you chose.","whyWrong":["No extra encryption is involved.","","Lifetimes are unchanged.","It is a TLS control."]},{"q":"Modern pinning pins what, exactly?","options":["The certificate file served by the host","The domain name in the certificate","The name of the issuing CA","A hash of the public key (SubjectPublicKeyInfo)"],"answer":3,"why":"Certificates are reissued routinely; if the key is carried across the renewal, a key pin survives it while a certificate pin does not.","whyWrong":["Every renewal replaces it, so the pin breaks.","The certificate already binds the name; pinning it adds nothing.","A name is not key material and is easy to match.",""]},{"q":"Why is pinning an intermediate usually preferred to pinning the leaf?","options":["A leaf certificate's public key cannot be hashed into a pin","An intermediate key is inherently stronger than a leaf key","It survives ordinary leaf rotation while still excluding every other CA","TLS 1.3 requires any pin to name an intermediate rather than a leaf certificate"],"answer":2,"why":"Leaf pinning makes every certificate renewal a coordinated client release; intermediate pinning keeps the narrowing without that coupling.","whyWrong":["It hashes fine. That is exactly how leaf pinning works.","Neither is stronger. It is the same kind of key material.","","TLS says nothing about pinning, at any version."]},{"q":"You pin a single leaf key in a mobile app and then rotate the key. What happens?","options":["The app fetches the new pin automatically over the air","Clients fall back to normal CA validation and carry on","Only new installs are affected, while the existing ones keep working fine","Installed clients refuse to connect, and nothing server-side fixes it"],"answer":3,"why":"A failed pin does not degrade gracefully, and you cannot push an update through a connection the client will not make. This is why backup pins exist.","whyWrong":["It cannot reach you to fetch anything; the connection fails first.","Falling back would defeat the point of pinning.","Existing installs are exactly what breaks.",""]},{"q":"Why was HTTP Public Key Pinning (HPKP) removed from browsers?","options":["It was replaced by a safer header that does the same job in every modern browser","Sites bricked themselves, and a hostile pin could deny service to a domain","Browsers dropped the TLS extension the header depended on","It was only ever a draft, and no browser ever implemented it"],"answer":1,"why":"The failure mode was severe and irreversible for the site's own users, which is why pinning survives in applications you control rather than in browsers.","whyWrong":["Nothing equivalent replaced it. Certificate Transparency covers different ground.","","No extension went away, and browsers still speak TLS.","It shipped in Chrome and Firefox, then was removed later."]}],"s2sx":[{"q":"What problem does token exchange exist to solve?","options":["Making tokens smaller, by swapping a large assertion for a compact reference the API can resolve","Encrypting tokens in transit, so an intermediary in the call chain cannot read the claims inside","Letting users authenticate without a password, by exchanging a device signal for a session token","A service needs to call another service on a user's behalf, but the token it holds was minted for itself"],"answer":3,"why":"The token you were handed is addressed to you. The next service needs one addressed to it, still tied to the same user.","whyWrong":["Size is not the concern.","Transport security is TLS's job.","That is authentication, a different protocol.",""]},{"q":"In an exchange request, which parameter says WHO the work is being done for?","options":["subject_token","actor_token","audience","scope"],"answer":0,"why":"The subject token identifies the principal the new token will be about, usually the user whose request started everything.","whyWrong":["","The actor token says who is ASKING.","audience says where the new token may be used.","scope says what it may do."]},{"q":"Why is forwarding the user's original token to every downstream service a bad idea?","options":["The user would have to log in again, because forwarding consumes the token's single permitted use","Every service then holds a credential that works at every other service, so one compromise spreads sideways","Tokens cannot be presented twice, so the second service in the chain would be rejected outright","It is slower, since every downstream hop has to validate a token it did not request itself"],"answer":1,"why":"An over-broad token turns a single compromised workload into access to everything the user could reach, and it defeats audience validation.","whyWrong":["The user is not involved.","","Nothing prevents re-sending a token.","Forwarding is the fast option; that is its appeal."]},{"q":"A token issued by delegation differs from one issued by impersonation in what way?","options":["Delegation requires PKCE on the exchange request, impersonation does not","Delegation tokens are issued with longer lifetimes than impersonation ones","Impersonation tokens are encrypted so the acting service stays hidden","Delegation records the acting service in an act claim; impersonation records only the user"],"answer":3,"why":"Delegation keeps both parties visible, so downstream services and audit logs know the user AND which service acted. Impersonation is indistinguishable from the user acting directly.","whyWrong":["PKCE belongs to the authorization code flow and plays no part in token exchange.","Lifetime is set by policy and does not follow from the mode you picked.","Neither mode encrypts by default. Impersonation hides the actor by omitting it.",""]},{"q":"Service A exchanges a user token and calls B; B exchanges again and calls C. In the token C receives, what should the claims look like?","options":["sub is service A, with act naming service B","sub is still the user, act names B with A nested inside","sub is service B, and the user is lost","Each hop replaces act, so only B appears"],"answer":1,"why":"The subject stays the original user at every hop, and each new actor nests the previous one, so the chain reads most-recent-first and the whole path stays attributable.","whyWrong":["The subject stays the user; a service never takes the sub slot.","","Losing the user defeats the purpose of the exchange.","Replacing act erases the middle of the chain, a common implementation bug."]}],"s2stxn":[{"q":"What is a Transaction Token?","options":["A replacement for TLS between services, carrying the confidentiality the transport would provide","A short-lived signed JWT carrying the identity and context of one request through an internal call chain","An encrypted refresh token, issued so that intermediaries in the call chain cannot read it","A long-lived credential for machine-to-machine calls"],"answer":1,"why":"It is minted at the boundary for one transaction and passed along every internal hop that transaction causes.","whyWrong":["It complements transport security rather than replacing it.","","No encryption is implied, and refresh tokens are explicitly excluded.","They live for minutes and are bound to one transaction."]},{"q":"In a Txn-Token, what does the aud claim identify?","options":["The workload that requested it","The single service that may accept it","The user it describes","The trust domain in which it is valid"],"answer":3,"why":"Deliberately unlike an access token: the audience is a whole trust domain, so many workloads inside the boundary may accept it and nothing outside may.","whyWrong":["That is req_wl.","That is how an access token's audience works, and this inverts it.","That is sub.",""]},{"q":"What is the relationship between RFC 8693 and Transaction Tokens?","options":["Txn-Tokens replaced RFC 8693 once the draft reached last call","They are one specification, published under two different names","RFC 8693 token exchange is how you request a Txn-Token from the TTS","They are unrelated, since Txn-Tokens use their own request format"],"answer":2,"why":"A Txn-Token is obtained with an ordinary token-exchange request whose requested_token_type names the txn_token type. Exchange is the how; the transaction token is the what.","whyWrong":["RFC 8693 is unchanged and still used for plenty of other exchanges.","RFC 8693 is a finished RFC. Transaction tokens are a separate draft.","","The request is an ordinary token exchange, with requested_token_type naming the type."]},{"q":"A compromised service in the middle of a call chain changes the transfer amount before calling the next service. Which part of the design catches it?","options":["The txn claim, which pins the amount for the whole chain","The signed tctx claim carrying the call parameters","Mutual TLS between the services","The exp claim, since a short lifetime blocks tampering"],"answer":1,"why":"tctx holds the parameters that must not change, and it is covered by the TTS's signature, so a downstream workload can detect that what it was asked to do differs from what the customer requested.","whyWrong":["txn only correlates the calls, it carries no amount to pin.","","mTLS authenticates the channel, and a compromised service has a legitimate one.","exp bounds how long the token lives, not what it says."]},{"q":"Which statement about Txn-Tokens is accurate?","options":["Once signed, any trust domain that trusts the issuer key will accept them","They do not prevent replay inside the trust domain during their lifetime","They can be refreshed with a refresh token when the original expires","They carry a one-time nonce that blocks replay inside the trust domain"],"answer":1,"why":"The draft is explicit that replay within the domain and lifetime remains possible, so short expiry and narrow scope are the mitigations rather than an afterthought.","whyWrong":["The aud claim pins each token to a single trust domain, so a neighboring domain rejects it.","","There is no refresh flow here; the service asks the token service for a fresh one.","There is no nonce. Replay inside the domain stays possible, which is why lifetimes are short."]}],"idf0":[{"q":"What does IdP stand for?","options":["Identity Provider","Internal Data Protocol","Identity Policy","Integrated Directory"],"answer":0,"why":"The IdP is the authority that authenticates users and issues tokens or assertions.","whyWrong":["","That is not what IdP means.","A policy is a rule set, not the authenticating authority.","A directory stores users; the IdP authenticates them."]},{"q":"What does OIDC stand for?","options":["OAuth Identity Config","Online ID Check","OpenID Connect","Open Identity Credential"],"answer":2,"why":"OpenID Connect is the authentication layer built on top of OAuth 2.0.","whyWrong":["Not a real term.","Not a real term.","","Not a real term."]},{"q":"What does MFA mean?","options":["Managed Federation Access","Master File Access","Multi-Factor Authentication","Multi-Field Authorization"],"answer":2,"why":"MFA requires two or more independent factors to sign in.","whyWrong":["Made up.","Made up.","","Made up."]},{"q":"What does SSO mean?","options":["Session Storage Object","Signed Security Officer","Secure Socket Option","Single Sign-On"],"answer":3,"why":"Single Sign-On lets one login reach many applications.","whyWrong":["Not a real term.","Not a real term.","Not a real term.",""]}],"idffed":[{"q":"In federation, which party authenticates the user and issues the signed proof?","options":["The API gateway","Identity Provider (IdP)","The browser","Relying Party (RP)"],"answer":1,"why":"The IdP vouches for the user; everyone else relies on that.","whyWrong":["A gateway routes traffic; it does not authenticate the user.","","The browser only carries the proof.","The RP relies on the proof, it does not create it."]},{"q":"What does the Service Provider / Relying Party do?","options":["Relies on the IdP's proof instead of a password","Signs the token it sends to the IdP","Stores a copy of every user's password","Runs the MFA prompt for the user"],"answer":0,"why":"The whole point of federation is that the app trusts the IdP rather than holding passwords.","whyWrong":["","The IdP signs the assertion; the RP only verifies it.","Not holding passwords is the reason to federate.","Authentication and MFA happen at the IdP."]},{"q":"Why can a random website not fake \"Google says this is you\"?","options":["It cannot be prevented, only detected afterwards","The browser blocks any token that arrives from the wrong origin","The RP only accepts tokens signed by the pre-trusted IdP key","Google emails the user a confirmation for each login"],"answer":2,"why":"Trust is set up in advance with the IdP public keys, so only real signatures verify.","whyWrong":["Signature verification prevents it outright.","The browser does not verify signatures; the RP does.","","No email is involved in verifying a token."]}],"idffed2":[{"q":"Which piece is the authority that authenticates users?","options":["Identity Provider (IdP)","Relying Party","Access token","JWKS endpoint"],"answer":0,"why":"The IdP is the authenticating authority.","whyWrong":["","The relying party consumes the proof.","A token is the proof, not the authority.","JWKS just publishes the keys."]},{"q":"What is the \"proof\" passed in federation?","options":["A signed token or assertion","A username and password","A TLS certificate","A session cookie only"],"answer":0,"why":"The IdP issues a signed token (OIDC) or assertion (SAML) the app can verify.","whyWrong":["","Passwords are what federation avoids sending around.","A certificate proves a server, not the user login.","A cookie tracks a local session, it is not the federated proof."]},{"q":"Where does the IdP publish the keys used to verify its tokens?","options":["In the user cookie","A JWKS endpoint / SAML metadata","Inside the access token","In the client secret"],"answer":1,"why":"The IdP publishes public keys at JWKS (OIDC) or in metadata (SAML) so relying parties can verify signatures.","whyWrong":["Cookies do not carry IdP keys.","","The token carries a signature, not the verification key.","The client secret authenticates the app, it is not the IdP signing key."]}],"am2":[{"q":"A password is which kind of authentication factor?","options":["Something you know (knowledge)","Somewhere you are","Something you are (inherence)","Something you have (possession)"],"answer":0,"why":"A password is knowledge, a secret you remember.","whyWrong":["","Location is a context signal, not this factor.","Inherence is a biometric.","Possession is a device or key you hold."]},{"q":"A one-time code from an authenticator app is which factor?","options":["Something you are","Something you know","A recovery factor","Something you have (possession)"],"answer":3,"why":"The code comes from a device you possess.","whyWrong":["Inherence is a biometric.","Knowledge is a memorized secret.","Recovery is a flow, not a factor category.",""]},{"q":"A fingerprint is which factor?","options":["Something you know","Somewhere you are (location)","Something you have","Something you are (inherence)"],"answer":3,"why":"A biometric is inherence, something you are.","whyWrong":["Knowledge is memorized.","Location is a context signal, not a biometric.","Possession is a held device.",""]},{"q":"True MFA combines factors from…","options":["one strong factor","two or more different categories","the same category twice","two passwords"],"answer":1,"why":"The categories must differ, so stealing one (a password) is not enough.","whyWrong":["One factor, however strong, is single-factor.","","Same category twice is not multi-factor.","Two passwords are both knowledge, still one factor type."]}],"am6":[{"q":"Which NIST scale measures how strongly the real-world identity was proofed at enrollment?","options":["IAL (Identity Assurance Level)","TLS level","AAL (Authenticator Assurance Level)","FAL (Federation Assurance Level)"],"answer":0,"why":"IAL is about identity proofing.","whyWrong":["","TLS is transport security, not an identity scale.","AAL is about login strength, not proofing.","FAL is about how the assertion is protected."]},{"q":"Which scale measures how strong the login authentication is?","options":["AAL","MFA-2","FAL","IAL"],"answer":0,"why":"AAL rates authenticator strength (single factor, MFA, hardware).","whyWrong":["","Not a real scale.","FAL rates the federated assertion.","IAL rates identity proofing."]},{"q":"MFA corresponds to which authenticator level?","options":["AAL2","IAL2","AAL1","AAL3"],"answer":0,"why":"AAL2 requires two factors (MFA).","whyWrong":["","IAL2 is a proofing level, not an authenticator level.","AAL1 is a single factor.","AAL3 additionally requires a hardware, phishing-resistant authenticator."]},{"q":"A hardware, phishing-resistant authenticator (security key) is…","options":["AAL3","AAL1","FAL1","IAL1"],"answer":0,"why":"AAL3 is the highest authenticator level and requires hardware-backed, phishing-resistant auth.","whyWrong":["","AAL1 is single factor.","FAL is about assertions, not authenticators.","IAL1 is self-asserted identity, unrelated."]}],"am8":[{"q":"Creating a new passkey key pair for a site is which WebAuthn ceremony?","options":["Authentication (assertion)","Recovery","Registration (attestation)","Rotation"],"answer":2,"why":"Registration creates the key pair and may attest the authenticator.","whyWrong":["Authentication signs a challenge with an existing key.","Recovery is a separate account flow.","","Rotation is not a WebAuthn ceremony."]},{"q":"Signing a server challenge to log in is which ceremony?","options":["Enrollment","Provisioning","Authentication (assertion)","Registration (attestation)"],"answer":2,"why":"Authentication has the authenticator sign a fresh challenge (an assertion).","whyWrong":["Enrollment is another word for registration, not login.","Provisioning creates accounts, not this signature.","","Registration is the one-time key-pair creation."]},{"q":"In WebAuthn, the private key…","options":["is stored on the server","is the client secret","is emailed to the user","never leaves the device"],"answer":3,"why":"The private key stays in the device, which is what makes passkeys phishing-resistant.","whyWrong":["The server only stores the public key.","That is an OAuth concept, not a passkey.","Keys are never emailed.",""]}],"az2":[{"q":"In RBAC, permissions are granted to a user through…","options":["the request path","the session id","their IP address","roles"],"answer":3,"why":"Users get roles; roles carry permissions.","whyWrong":["The path is what is protected, not how access is granted.","A session id identifies a login, not permissions.","IP is a network detail, not an RBAC grant.",""]},{"q":"A \"viewer\" role typically has which permission?","options":["read","write","admin","read, write, delete"],"answer":0,"why":"A viewer can read only, least privilege.","whyWrong":["","Viewers do not write.","Admin is the most powerful role, not a viewer.","That is an admin-level set."]},{"q":"Which principle says a role should hold only the access it truly needs?","options":["Separation of duties","Defense in depth","Fail-closed","Least privilege"],"answer":3,"why":"Least privilege grants the minimum access required.","whyWrong":["Separation of duties splits conflicting powers between people.","Defense in depth is layered controls.","Fail-closed is a default-deny behavior, not this principle.",""]}],"oaclient":[{"q":"A single-page app (SPA) is which kind of OAuth client?","options":["A resource server","Public (uses PKCE, no secret)","An identity provider","Confidential, uses a client secret"],"answer":1,"why":"A SPA cannot hide a secret, so it is a public client and uses PKCE.","whyWrong":["The resource server is the API, not the app.","","The IdP is the authority, not the app.","It cannot keep a secret, so it is not confidential."]},{"q":"How does a server-side (confidential) client authenticate to the token endpoint?","options":["With the user password","It cannot authenticate","With a client secret or a key","With a session cookie"],"answer":2,"why":"Confidential clients prove themselves with a secret or an asymmetric key.","whyWrong":["The user password is never used by the client this way.","Confidential clients absolutely authenticate.","","Cookies are for browser sessions, not client auth."]},{"q":"Which client authentication method uses no shared secret?","options":["private_key_jwt (asymmetric key)","client_secret_post","HTTP Basic with the password","client_secret_basic"],"answer":0,"why":"private_key_jwt signs with a private key; the server verifies with the public key, nothing shared to leak.","whyWrong":["","Uses a shared secret.","Uses a password, the weakest option.","Uses a shared secret."]},{"q":"Where should a client_secret be stored?","options":["In localStorage, and cleared as soon as the user signs out","In the SPA bundle, minified so it is hard to pick out by hand","A secret manager or server environment, never in front-end code","In the redirect URL, sent over TLS to the registered callback"],"answer":2,"why":"Anything shipped to the browser is public, so secrets stay server-side.","whyWrong":["Any script on the page can read localStorage, and XSS is that script.","The bundle is downloadable by anyone, and minification is not concealment.","","URLs land in server logs, history and referrers, TLS or not."]}],"oa10":[{"q":"Best flow for a web or mobile app acting for a user?","options":["Authorization Code + PKCE","Client Credentials","Implicit","ROPC (password)"],"answer":0,"why":"Authorization Code with PKCE is the modern default for user-facing apps.","whyWrong":["","Client Credentials is for machines with no user.","Implicit is deprecated.","ROPC (password grant) is deprecated."]},{"q":"Best flow for machine-to-machine (no user present)?","options":["Client Credentials","Implicit","Authorization Code","Device flow"],"answer":0,"why":"Client Credentials authenticates the service itself, with no user.","whyWrong":["","Implicit is deprecated and user-facing.","Authorization Code needs a user at a browser.","Device flow is for input-limited devices with a user."]},{"q":"Which grant is deprecated and should be avoided?","options":["Authorization Code + PKCE","Implicit / ROPC","Client Credentials","Refresh"],"answer":1,"why":"Implicit and ROPC are discouraged by the OAuth Security BCP.","whyWrong":["This is the recommended flow.","","Still valid for M2M.","Refresh tokens are still used (with rotation)."]}],"s2s6":[{"q":"Inside a Kubernetes cluster or service mesh, how do services usually prove identity to each other?","options":["IP allowlists on the pod network","No auth at all once traffic is inside the cluster","A shared password injected through env vars","mTLS with workload certificates (e.g. SPIFFE/SPIRE)"],"answer":3,"why":"Each workload gets a short-lived certificate and services verify each other with mutual TLS.","whyWrong":["IP can be spoofed and is not identity.","Zero trust means never assuming the network is safe.","Shared static secrets are exactly what this avoids.",""]},{"q":"Machine-to-machine access to an external API is typically obtained via…","options":["a hardcoded API key in the repo","the user session cookie","OAuth client-credentials tokens","SAML browser SSO"],"answer":2,"why":"A service gets its own token from a minting authority with the client-credentials grant.","whyWrong":["Hardcoded keys leak and cannot rotate cleanly.","There is no user session in M2M.","","SAML browser SSO needs a human at a browser."]},{"q":"Zero trust means…","options":["trusting a caller once its source IP is on the approved list","authenticating once at the perimeter and trusting every later call in the session","verify identity and authorize every request, regardless of network location","treating anything inside the firewall as already authorized"],"answer":2,"why":"Never trust by location; authenticate and authorize each call.","whyWrong":["An IP is not an identity, and it is trivially spoofed or reused.","That is the model zero trust exists to replace.","","That is the old perimeter model, and one foothold defeats it."]}],"ei2":[{"q":"In Kerberos, what does the Authentication Service (AS) issue?","options":["A refresh token","A Ticket-Granting Ticket (TGT)","A service ticket","A password"],"answer":1,"why":"The AS verifies you once and issues a TGT.","whyWrong":["Refresh tokens are an OAuth concept.","","The TGS issues service tickets, using your TGT.","Passwords are never handed out."]},{"q":"To reach a specific service you present the TGT to the TGS and receive…","options":["a SAML assertion","another TGT","a service ticket","your password back"],"answer":2,"why":"The TGS grants a per-service ticket so your password never reaches the service.","whyWrong":["SAML is a different protocol.","You already have the TGT; the TGS gives a service ticket.","","Passwords are not returned."]}],"ei3":[{"q":"A new hire (Joiner) should trigger which provisioning action?","options":["Deactivate the account","Create the account","Nothing","Update access"],"answer":1,"why":"Joiner creates the account and grants baseline access.","whyWrong":["Deactivate is the Leaver case.","","Onboarding must create access.","Update is the Mover case."]},{"q":"An employee who leaves (Leaver) should trigger…","options":["Nothing automatic","Only a password reset","Deactivate/delete the account","Create a new account"],"answer":2,"why":"Leaver deprovisions access, the biggest security win of automation.","whyWrong":["Leaving accounts active is a top breach cause.","A reset does not remove access.","","That is the Joiner case."]},{"q":"Which standard automates pushing these create/update/deactivate operations?","options":["SAML","LDAP browsing","SCIM","OAuth"],"answer":2,"why":"SCIM is the provisioning API that syncs accounts across systems.","whyWrong":["SAML carries login assertions, not provisioning.","LDAP is a directory query protocol, not the provisioning push.","","OAuth is authorization for API access."]}],"ei4b":[{"q":"Workforce with an HR system of record is best served by…","options":["JIT only","SCIM pre-provisioning","Manual creation","No provisioning"],"answer":1,"why":"SCIM keeps accounts in sync from the HR source and supports clean deprovisioning.","whyWrong":["JIT alone will not deprovision leavers.","","Manual does not scale for a workforce.","Some provisioning is required."]},{"q":"A huge, unpredictable external / customer population is best served by…","options":["Manual creation","JIT provisioning","Pre-importing every user","LDAP sync"],"answer":1,"why":"JIT creates the account on first login, so you never pre-import millions of users.","whyWrong":["Manual cannot scale to consumers.","","Pre-importing millions is impractical.","LDAP sync fits a directory-backed workforce, not open consumers."]},{"q":"The classic gap with JIT provisioning is that it…","options":["requires SAML and cannot run over OIDC","cannot create accounts without a prior invite","is slower than manual provisioning at scale","creates accounts but does not deprovision them"],"answer":3,"why":"JIT provisions on first login but never removes access, so pair it with SCIM or reviews.","whyWrong":["JIT works with OIDC or SAML.","The first login is the trigger, no invite is needed.","JIT is far faster than manual.",""]}],"biz":[{"q":"Netflix serving individual consumers is an example of…","options":["B2C","B2B","B2B2C","P2P"],"answer":0,"why":"Selling directly to individual consumers is B2C.","whyWrong":["","B2B serves other businesses.","B2B2C serves a business that serves its own consumers.","P2P is not a business model here."]},{"q":"Salesforce serving other companies (each a tenant) is…","options":["B2B2C","B2C","C2C","B2B"],"answer":3,"why":"Selling to businesses, whose employees are the users, is B2B.","whyWrong":["B2B2C adds the tenant serving its own consumers.","B2C is direct-to-consumer.","C2C is consumer-to-consumer.",""]},{"q":"Shopify (merchants who serve their own shoppers through the platform) is…","options":["B2C","B2B2C","B2G","B2B"],"answer":1,"why":"You serve a business and, through it, that business own consumers, two identity layers.","whyWrong":["B2C is direct to consumers only.","","B2G is business-to-government.","B2B stops at the business tenant."]},{"q":"Which models are inherently multi-tenant?","options":["B2B and B2B2C","B2C only","none","B2C and B2B"],"answer":0,"why":"Serving businesses means each customer is an isolated tenant.","whyWrong":["","B2C is single-population, not tenant-based.","Multi-tenancy is central to B2B and B2B2C.","B2C is not multi-tenant."]}],"ao3":[{"q":"PAR (Pushed Authorization Requests) improves security by…","options":["encrypting the authorization server's client database at rest","hashing user passwords with a stronger algorithm at the token endpoint","rotating the refresh token every time the client redeems one","sending the request parameters to the server first, off the browser URL"],"answer":3,"why":"PAR moves sensitive parameters off the front channel into a back-channel push.","whyWrong":["Storage encryption is a separate control. PAR never touches the database.","Password storage is a different layer, and PAR runs before any credential check.","That is refresh-token rotation, a separate feature from PAR.",""]},{"q":"RAR (Rich Authorization Requests) provides…","options":["mutual TLS between the client and the authorization server","a faster token endpoint by pre-resolving scopes at registration","a new password hashing scheme for resource owner credentials","fine-grained, structured authorization details beyond coarse scopes"],"answer":3,"why":"RAR replaces blunt scopes with structured detail like \"transfer up to 500 from account X\".","whyWrong":["mTLS authenticates the client. It says nothing about what was authorized.","RAR is about detail, not speed, and it adds work rather than removing it.","Nothing in RAR touches passwords.",""]},{"q":"DPoP is about…","options":["sender-constrained (proof-of-possession) tokens","the design of user consent screens","compressing tokens on the wire","how passwords are stored at rest"],"answer":0,"why":"DPoP binds a token to a key the client must prove, so a stolen token is useless.","whyWrong":["","Consent is an authorization concern, not DPoP.","DPoP adds a proof; it makes requests bigger.","No password is involved anywhere in DPoP."]}],"ao5":[{"q":"Which defense stops CSRF on the OAuth redirect?","options":["A nonce","The state parameter","Exact redirect_uri matching","PKCE"],"answer":1,"why":"The state parameter is a random value the client checks on return, blocking CSRF.","whyWrong":["nonce defends ID-token replay, not the redirect CSRF.","","Exact redirect_uri defends open redirect / mix-up.","PKCE defends code interception."]},{"q":"Which defends against authorization-code interception by public clients?","options":["PKCE","CORS","nonce","state"],"answer":0,"why":"PKCE ties the code to a secret only the real client holds, so a stolen code is useless.","whyWrong":["","CORS is a browser policy, not this defense.","nonce defends replay of ID tokens.","state defends CSRF."]},{"q":"Which defends against replay of a token or ID token?","options":["exact redirect_uri","state","A one-time nonce","a longer secret"],"answer":2,"why":"A nonce (plus short expiry) makes a captured token single-use.","whyWrong":["redirect_uri matching defends open redirect.","state defends CSRF on the redirect.","","Length alone does not stop replay."]},{"q":"Open redirect / mix-up is mitigated by…","options":["registering and matching exact redirect_uri values","issuing longer authorization codes","allowing wildcards in the redirect_uri path","disabling PKCE for confidential clients"],"answer":0,"why":"Only exact, pre-registered redirect URIs should be accepted.","whyWrong":["","Code length does nothing about where the code is sent.","Wildcards are how the attack gets in.","Dropping PKCE removes a defense and adds none."]}],"ig5":[{"q":"CIAM (Customer IAM) primarily serves…","options":["service accounts","customers / consumers","admins only","employees"],"answer":1,"why":"CIAM is about external consumers, optimizing self-service and scale.","whyWrong":["Service accounts are machine identity.","","Admins are a small subset, not the focus.","Employees are workforce IAM."]},{"q":"Workforce IAM primarily serves…","options":["IoT devices","external customers","the public","employees and contractors"],"answer":3,"why":"Workforce IAM governs staff, emphasizing control and deprovisioning.","whyWrong":["Devices are workload/machine identity.","Customers are CIAM.","The public is CIAM territory.",""]}],"idcap":[{"q":"In the auth lifecycle, the \"register\" step should…","options":["hash the password (never store plaintext)","revoke the session cookie","validate the access token","issue an access and refresh token pair"],"answer":0,"why":"Registration stores a slow, salted hash of the password.","whyWrong":["","Revoking is logout.","Validation happens on each request.","Issuing tokens happens at login."]},{"q":"On \"login\", the server…","options":["deletes the account","hashes the password","rotates signing keys","issues tokens (access + refresh)"],"answer":3,"why":"After verifying credentials, login mints the tokens.","whyWrong":["Login does not delete anything.","Hashing is a registration step.","Key rotation is an operational task, not login.",""]},{"q":"On each \"request\", the server…","options":["validates the access token, then authorizes the action","re-hashes the password sent with the request","creates the account if it does not exist","issues a new refresh token with the response"],"answer":0,"why":"Every request checks the token (iss/aud/exp) and then the permission.","whyWrong":["","No password travels with an ordinary API request.","Account creation happens once, at signup.","Refresh tokens come from the token endpoint, not every response."]},{"q":"On \"logout\", the server…","options":["issues a token","revokes the session","creates a session","hashes a password"],"answer":1,"why":"Logout must end the session server-side, not just drop the cookie.","whyWrong":["Tokens are issued at login.","","Creating a session is login.","Hashing is registration."]}],"am9":[{"q":"Where do most account breaches actually come from?","options":["Tricking a support agent into a reset","Zero-day exploits against the login service","Credential stuffing with passwords leaked elsewhere","Brute-forcing strong passwords"],"answer":2,"why":"Attackers replay username/password pairs leaked from other sites, betting on reuse, cheap and automated.","whyWrong":["That happens, but it is a small share of takeovers.","Zero-days are rare and costly next to stuffing lists.","","Brute-forcing strong passwords is slow and usually rate-limited."]},{"q":"Which single control makes a leaked password insufficient on its own?","options":["A longer password policy","MFA","Login email notifications","Security questions"],"answer":1,"why":"With MFA, a correct-but-leaked password still cannot complete the login.","whyWrong":["Length does not help once the exact password has leaked.","","Notifications warn after the fact; they do not block.","Knowledge answers are also leakable and guessable."]},{"q":"Checking a new password against known-breached lists is done safely with…","options":["downloading every breach in full","sending the full password to a third party","an email round-trip","k-anonymity (only a hash prefix leaves you)"],"answer":3,"why":"Services like Have I Been Pwned use k-anonymity, so the password itself never leaves your server.","whyWrong":["Impractical and unnecessary.","You must never send the full password.","Unrelated to breach checks.",""]},{"q":"A flood of failed logins across many accounts is best stopped by…","options":["longer sessions","taking the site offline","rate limiting + bot detection","a CAPTCHA on every page"],"answer":2,"why":"Throttle the automation and detect bots; keep humans unblocked.","whyWrong":["Unrelated to the attack.","That is denial of service to yourself.","","CAPTCHA everywhere punishes real users, a last resort."]}],"iddid":[{"q":"In decentralized identity, where do your credentials live?","options":["On a central IdP server","In your own wallet","Only in the browser cache","In a shared database"],"answer":1,"why":"The holder keeps Verifiable Credentials in a wallet and presents them directly.","whyWrong":["There is no central IdP mediating each sign-in.","","A wallet is durable storage, not just cache.","They are not held in a shared database."]},{"q":"A Verifiable Credential is…","options":["a signed, tamper-evident claim from an issuer","an allowlist of IP addresses a holder may use","a session cookie issued after a successful login","a username and password held by the issuer"],"answer":0,"why":"A VC is a cryptographically signed claim (e.g. \"over 18\") you can present and others can verify.","whyWrong":["","A VC carries a claim about a subject, not a network rule.","A cookie is a local session handle and proves nothing off that site.","A VC is presented, not typed in, and it holds no secret."]},{"q":"Selective disclosure lets you…","options":["skip verifying the credential entirely","reset your password at the issuing authority","share your whole profile in a single presentation","prove you are over 18 without revealing your birthdate"],"answer":3,"why":"Selective disclosure / zero-knowledge proofs reveal only the fact needed, nothing more.","whyWrong":["Verification still happens on the disclosed fact.","Unrelated.","That is the opposite of the goal.",""]},{"q":"Compared with federation, decentralized identity…","options":["has no central IdP that sees every login","cannot be verified without the issuer online","is exactly the same thing","still requires a central IdP"],"answer":0,"why":"No central authority mediates each sign-in, which improves privacy and resilience.","whyWrong":["","A verifier checks the signature without contacting the issuer.","It is a distinct model from federation.","It removes the central IdP."]}],"igaudit":[{"q":"To prove who did what and when, you need…","options":["a bigger cache","a faster CPU","more RAM","an immutable audit log"],"answer":3,"why":"An append-only, tamper-evident audit trail is the evidence auditors and investigators need.","whyWrong":["Caching is unrelated.","Speed is unrelated to auditability.","Hardware does not make actions provable.",""]},{"q":"Real-time detection of suspicious login patterns is the job of…","options":["the DNS resolver","the password hasher","a SIEM","the stylesheet"],"answer":2,"why":"A SIEM ingests the logs and alerts on patterns like failed-login bursts or a new admin.","whyWrong":["Unrelated.","Hashing protects stored passwords, not detection.","","Unrelated."]},{"q":"Which must NEVER appear in identity logs?","options":["full tokens or secrets","the timestamp","the user id","the action name"],"answer":0,"why":"Logging secrets or full tokens turns your logs into a breach; log identifiers and metadata only.","whyWrong":["","Timestamps are required.","The subject id is expected and useful.","The action is the whole point of the log."]},{"q":"Frameworks like SOC 2 and NIST 800-63 map their controls to identity practices such as…","options":["the color scheme of the admin console","the database vendor you run in production","MFA, least privilege, deprovisioning, audit logs","the wording of your marketing site"],"answer":2,"why":"Those frameworks require exactly these identity controls, evidenced by your audit trail.","whyWrong":["Appearance is not a control anyone audits.","No framework names a vendor; controls are about practice.","","Marketing text is not evidence of anything."]}],"idflife":[{"q":"Identity proofing happens at which point?","options":["Never, it is automatic","Once, before the account exists","Only when the password is reset","Every time the user logs in"],"answer":1,"why":"Proofing establishes who the person is in the real world, before an account is created. Authentication is the repeated step afterwards.","whyWrong":["It is a deliberate process, not something that happens by itself.","","A reset re-binds a credential; it does not re-establish the real-world identity.","That is authentication (proofing is not repeated per login.)"]},{"q":"What is the difference between an authenticator and a credential?","options":["A credential is always a password, stored hashed by the server","An authenticator is always hardware, such as a key or a phone","They are two words for the same thing in every NIST document","An authenticator is what you hold; a credential is the stored binding"],"answer":3,"why":"You possess an authenticator (password, passkey, security key); the system stores a credential recording that it belongs to your account.","whyWrong":["A password is one kind of authenticator, and a credential is the binding record.","Knowledge and biometric factors count as authenticators too.","NIST separates them deliberately, because the attacks against each differ.",""]},{"q":"An organization fails an audit for accounts belonging to people who left years ago. Which lifecycle step broke?","options":["Credential binding","Identity proofing","Authentication","Deprovisioning"],"answer":3,"why":"Deprovisioning, the leaver step, is the one most often skipped, leaving orphaned accounts with live access.","whyWrong":["Binding worked; that is why the accounts still function.","Proofing was fine; these were real employees.","Authentication is working correctly, which is exactly the problem.",""]},{"q":"Why should an application key its user records on a stable internal id rather than an email address?","options":["Email addresses are far too long to index efficiently as a primary key","It makes no practical difference which one you key on","Emails are encrypted in the directory and cannot be keyed","Email addresses can change or be reassigned to a different person"],"answer":3,"why":"An identifier that can be reassigned will eventually attach one person’s history to another.","whyWrong":["Length is not the issue; indexes handle them fine.","It matters a great deal, and the bill usually arrives years later.","Directory emails are not encrypted, and encryption would not help.",""]},{"q":"One person has a work account, a Google account and a customer account at the same shop. How many people is that?","options":["Three","Three identities and no person","It depends on the protocol","One person with three accounts"],"answer":3,"why":"A person is not an account. One human routinely holds many accounts, each with its own identifier.","whyWrong":["Accounts are not people; conflating them is the mistake this lesson exists to prevent.","There is very much a person; the accounts describe them.","The distinction holds regardless of protocol.",""]}],"idfclaim":[{"q":"What turns a stored attribute into a claim?","options":["Adding it to a database index","Encrypting it at rest in the store","Being asserted by a specific issuer, in transit","Storing it in a directory the app reads"],"answer":2,"why":"An attribute is a fact at rest; it becomes a claim when an issuer asserts it to someone else.","whyWrong":["Indexing is a storage detail.","Encryption changes who can read it, not what kind of fact it is.","","That is what makes it an attribute in the first place."]},{"q":"Which claim should an application treat as the identity of the user?","options":["sub","email","name","preferred_username"],"answer":0,"why":"sub is immutable and scoped to the issuer, so it never gets reassigned to a different person.","whyWrong":["","Email addresses get changed and reused.","Names are not unique and change frequently.","A username can be released and reassigned."]},{"q":"What does the aud claim tell a verifier?","options":["Which algorithm signed it","Who issued the token","When it expires","Who this token is intended for"],"answer":3,"why":"aud names the intended recipient. A token minted for a different API is not valid for yours.","whyWrong":["That is alg, in the header.","That is iss.","That is exp.",""]},{"q":"An IdP issues a token claiming \"role\":\"admin\", but roles are defined by your application. What should you conclude?","options":["The token is forged, since roles are yours to set","The claim is only as good as the issuer's authority","The user is an admin; the signature is valid","Any signed claim must be honored"],"answer":1,"why":"A valid signature proves who said it, not that they had any business saying it. Trust an issuer only for facts it is authoritative over.","whyWrong":["Nothing points to forgery; the signature is fine and the issue is authority.","","A valid signature does not confer authority over your application concepts.","Honoring every signed claim is how privilege escalation happens."]},{"q":"Why do federation integrations often authenticate successfully but create broken user records?","options":["Mismatched attribute mapping between the IdP and the app","Expired signing certificates on the IdP metadata","Slow networks truncating the assertion somewhere in transit","Weak passwords accepted at the identity provider"],"answer":0,"why":"Every system names attributes differently, and a wrong mapping still passes signature checks, the login works, the data lands in the wrong fields.","whyWrong":["","An expired certificate breaks the login itself, not just the record.","A truncated assertion fails the signature check outright.","Password strength has nothing to do with how attributes land."]}],"idftok":[{"q":"What is a token, in one sentence?","options":["A string standing for a verified fact, issued by an authority and presented later","The user's password, encrypted so it can travel safely over the wire","A hashing algorithm that turns a password into a fixed-length digest","A cookie set by the browser to keep a session alive between requests"],"answer":0,"why":"You prove yourself once and receive a string you present instead of proving yourself again.","whyWrong":["","A token carries no password. It stands in for a check that already happened.","That is a hash function, a building block used inside tokens rather than the token itself.","A cookie is one way to carry a token. Tokens also travel in headers and request bodies."]},{"q":"What makes a token \"opaque\"?","options":["It carries no readable meaning and must be looked up at the issuer","It cannot expire, since there is no expiry claim inside it to read","It is encrypted, so only the issuer can decrypt the claims inside","It is short enough to fit in a header without compression"],"answer":0,"why":"An opaque token is a random lookup key; the real data stays in the issuer store.","whyWrong":["","Expiry lives in the issuer's store, and an opaque token expires like any other.","There are no claims inside. An opaque token is usually just random bytes.","Length is incidental. A long random string is just as opaque."]},{"q":"Can anyone who intercepts a JWT read its claims?","options":["No, the signature encrypts the payload","Only if alg is none","Yes (base64url is encoding, not encryption)","Only with the private key"],"answer":2,"why":"The payload is merely base64url-encoded. The signature prevents tampering, not reading.","whyWrong":["A signature authenticates; it does not conceal.","The alg value has nothing to do with readability.","","No key is needed to decode; that is what JWE is for."]},{"q":"\"Access token\" and \"JWT\" describe…","options":["A role and a format, which vary independently","The same thing under two names","Two competing standards for the same job","Two parts of the same token"],"answer":0,"why":"Access token is the job the token does; JWT is how the bytes are arranged. An access token may be a JWT or opaque.","whyWrong":["","They answer different questions entirely.","They are not alternatives to each other.","A JWT is not a component of an access token."]},{"q":"Why is revocation harder with a structured token than an opaque one?","options":["Opaque tokens never expire, so revocation is the only exit","Structured tokens are too long to put in a revocation list you can index","Structured tokens carry no signature to check against","There is no central record to delete, it stays valid until it expires"],"answer":3,"why":"Self-contained tokens verify offline, so nothing central exists to invalidate. Short lifetimes plus refresh are the industry patch.","whyWrong":["They expire like anything else; being revocable is the real difference.","Length affects bandwidth, not whether you can revoke.","They are signed, which is what makes offline verification work.",""]},{"q":"A verifier reads the alg field from the token header and uses whatever it says. What is the flaw?","options":["It only becomes a problem when the token was signed with RSA","alg is optional, so the header may not carry one at all","An attacker can propose alg:none and talk it out of verifying","It is slow, since the verifier has to load a codec per token"],"answer":2,"why":"The algorithm must be pinned by the verifier in advance, never chosen by the token being checked.","whyWrong":["Every algorithm is affected, including a swap to HMAC.","alg is present. Obeying it is the problem, not its absence.","","Performance is not the concern here."]}],"idfcast":[{"q":"SAML says \"Service Provider\". What does OAuth/OIDC call the same box?","options":["Client (or Relying Party)","Authorization Server","Resource Server","Subject"],"answer":0,"why":"The app relying on someone else authentication is the SP in SAML and the client/RP in OIDC.","whyWrong":["","That is the IdP counterpart, the authority.","That is the protected API, which SAML has no equivalent for.","That is the person."]},{"q":"Who is the \"resource owner\" in OAuth?","options":["The API that stores and serves the data","The client application asking for access","The person who owns the data and can grant access","The company running the server"],"answer":2,"why":"The resource owner is the user whose data is at stake, and therefore the only one who can grant an app access to it.","whyWrong":["That is the resource server.","That is the client; the owner is the user it asks on behalf of.","","Ownership here is about the data subject, not the operator."]},{"q":"Which actor is the only one that mints tokens?","options":["The client","The resource server","The browser","The authorization server"],"answer":3,"why":"The authority issues tokens; clients present them and resource servers verify them.","whyWrong":["A client receives and presents tokens.","A resource server verifies and consumes them.","The browser only carries them.",""]},{"q":"\"Authenticate the client\" means…","options":["Prove which application is calling","Verify the token signature","Check the user password","Prove which person is logged in"],"answer":0,"why":"A client is a registered application with its own identity, separate from the user identity.","whyWrong":["","Signature verification is a separate step.","Passwords belong to users, not clients.","That is authenticating the user, a different question in the same flow."]},{"q":"How does an application learn an OIDC authority signing keys?","options":["They are derived from the client secret","From the discovery document jwks_uri","They are embedded in the access token","The user supplies them"],"answer":1,"why":"The authority publishes /.well-known/openid-configuration, which points at the JWKS holding its public keys.","whyWrong":["A client secret authenticates the app; it is unrelated to the issuer signing key.","","A token carries a signature, not the key needed to check it.","Users are never involved in key distribution."]}],"idf3":[{"q":"Single Sign-On is best described as…","options":["A user experience: one login event, many applications","A wire protocol, with its own defined set of request messages","A token format carried between applications","An encryption standard for session cookies"],"answer":0,"why":"SSO names an outcome the user perceives. It specifies no messages or actors, and can be produced several different ways.","whyWrong":["","There is no SSO protocol; SAML and OIDC are the protocols.","SSO is not a token; tokens are one means to the end.","Nothing about SSO is an encryption scheme."]},{"q":"Several apps under the same domain share one session cookie, so logging into one logs you into all. Is that SSO?","options":["Yes, and it needs no federation at all","No, SSO requires an IdP","Only if a token is involved","No, SSO requires SAML or OIDC"],"answer":0,"why":"It is genuine SSO, one login, many apps, achieved with cookie scope alone. This is why \"we need SSO\" must be followed by \"across what boundary?\"","whyWrong":["","An IdP is needed for federated SSO, not for SSO as such.","A session cookie is enough; no token is required.","Those are needed to cross an organizational boundary, not to achieve the experience."]},{"q":"An organization federates exactly one application. What do they gain?","options":["Central MFA and policy, instant deprovisioning, no stored password","Only a nicer login page, hosted by the IdP instead of the app","Only faster logins, since the app skips its own password check","Nothing at all, since one app cannot deliver sign-on-once"],"answer":0,"why":"The structural wins are the point of federation; SSO is the side effect that appears once you federate a second app.","whyWrong":["","Appearance is incidental. The wins are central policy and deprovisioning.","Federation usually adds a redirect, so it is not about speed.","The SSO benefit is absent, but central policy and deprovisioning are not."]},{"q":"Why is using an OAuth access token as proof of who the user is a vulnerability?","options":["Access tokens are unsigned, so a resource server has no way to confirm who actually issued one","Access tokens expire too quickly, so the identity they assert is stale by the time it is read","An access token authorizes an app to call an API; it makes no reliable statement about the user","It is not a problem, since the token was issued only after the user authenticated successfully"],"answer":2,"why":"OAuth answers \"may this app do this?\" not \"who are you?\": OpenID Connect adds the ID token precisely to answer the second question.","whyWrong":["Many are signed; signing does not make them identity statements.","Expiry is unrelated to the confusion.","","It is a well-documented class of vulnerability."]},{"q":"What distinguishes delegation from impersonation?","options":["They are the same thing, with the two names coming from different specifications describing one flow","Delegation is faster, since it avoids the extra assertion exchange that impersonation requires","Impersonation is always malicious, whereas delegation is the sanctioned form of the same mechanism","Delegation records both the app and the user; impersonation makes the app indistinguishable from the user"],"answer":3,"why":"In delegation the API can see a client acting for a user. In impersonation it sees only the user, which is more powerful and harder to audit.","whyWrong":["They are formalised as distinct modes in token exchange.","Speed is not the difference.","Impersonation is a legitimate, deliberate pattern, it just needs care.",""]},{"q":"Why is Single Logout so much less reliable than Single Sign-On?","options":["Logout was never standardized, so each vendor invented an incompatible mechanism of its own","Browsers block logout requests as third-party traffic, so most of them never reach the relying party","Logout messages are not encrypted, so intermediaries can drop them without either party noticing","The single login actually created many separate application sessions, and there is no reliable way to close them all"],"answer":3,"why":"SSO is a pleasant illusion over N real sessions; ending one does not end the rest.","whyWrong":["SLO is specified; it is the distributed state that makes it fragile.","Browsers do not block logout requests.","Encryption is not the obstacle.",""]}],"idfdauthn":[{"q":"In a design review, which single question tells you whether an integration is credential forwarding or federation?","options":["Whether TLS is enabled","Which protocol version is used","Where does the user type their password","How long the token lasts"],"answer":2,"why":"If the password is typed into a form your application renders, you are forwarding credentials, whatever the diagram claims.","whyWrong":["TLS protects transport; the app still holds the password.","Version numbers say nothing about credential exposure.","","Lifetime is a token property, not a credential-path property."]},{"q":"An app collects the username and password and performs an LDAP bind to verify them. What is the main security consequence?","options":["The app is inside the credential blast radius","LDAP is unencrypted","Nothing, this is the modern default","Passwords cannot be hashed"],"answer":0,"why":"The application holds plaintext passwords in memory and logs, so compromising the app compromises every password typed into it.","whyWrong":["","LDAP can run over TLS; that is a separate concern.","It is a legacy pattern, deprecated wherever a browser exists.","The directory still hashes them; exposure is at the app."]},{"q":"Why can credential forwarding not support passkeys or SSO?","options":["They require newer runtime versions than most legacy forwarding proxies are able to support","Both are proprietary, so a proxy cannot reimplement the ceremony without vendor cooperation","The authority never interacts with the user directly, and no reusable login session is created","Passkeys need a password first, so a forwarding proxy has nothing to present on the second step"],"answer":2,"why":"Passkeys need the authenticator to talk to the authority, and SSO needs a session at the authority for a second app to reuse. Forwarding creates neither.","whyWrong":["Runtime versions are irrelevant.","Both are open standards.","","Passkeys replace passwords rather than supplementing them."]},{"q":"Which delegated-authentication method is still defensible today?","options":["ROPC for new web apps, as long as the client is confidential","Credential forwarding for legacy protocols with no browser, like IMAP","Any method at all, provided the connection is protected by TLS","Embedded webviews, since the IdP page renders inside them normally"],"answer":1,"why":"Protocols with no browser cannot redirect. The mitigations are app-specific passwords or moving the protocol onto OAuth with SASL.","whyWrong":["The Security BCP removes ROPC outright, and client type does not rescue it.","","TLS protects the wire. It does nothing about an app that holds the password.","The host app can read everything typed into a webview, which is forwarding with extra steps."]},{"q":"Someone says \"we delegate authentication to Okta.\" What do they almost certainly mean?","options":["The app collects the password on its own login form and posts it to an Okta API endpoint","Federation, the user authenticates at Okta and the app receives a signed assertion","Okta issues each user a client certificate for the app to check","Okta holds a copy of the app's password file and syncs it nightly"],"answer":1,"why":"Both are literally delegated authentication, but the redirect style is what people mean, and it is the one that keeps the app out of the blast radius.","whyWrong":["That is credential forwarding, the pattern federation exists to avoid.","","Certificate authentication is a different mechanism, and rarely what people mean.","No password file is being synced. The app never handles credentials at all."]}],"idfdauthz":[{"q":"Before OAuth, an app needing your cloud photos asked for your cloud password. Which problem does delegated authorization NOT solve?","options":["The need to authenticate at all","Unlimited scope of access","Access that never expires","No way to revoke one app"],"answer":0,"why":"Delegation bounds, expires and revokes app access, but somebody still has to authenticate the user first.","whyWrong":["","Scopes bound it.","exp expires it.","Revoking the grant removes one app."]},{"q":"What is the difference between revoking a token and revoking a grant?","options":["They are identical, since revoking either one invalidates the same underlying authorization record","Tokens outlive grants, so revoking the grant leaves issued tokens working until they lapse","A token is one credential; the grant is the recorded permission, so revoking it also stops refreshes","Grants cannot be revoked at all, so the only recourse is to wait for the tokens to expire"],"answer":2,"why":"\"Remove this app access\" means deleting the grant, otherwise the refresh token quietly mints new access tokens.","whyWrong":["They operate at different levels.","Grants outlive tokens, not the reverse.","","Grants are exactly what the user removes."]},{"q":"A token carries scope photos:read. Ada uses it to request Bob photos. What should happen?","options":["Allowed, the scope grants read access to any photo","Denied, the scope bounds the app, not Ada's rights","Denied, because photos:read is read-only","Allowed if Bob account is public"],"answer":1,"why":"Both checks must pass: what the app was allowed to ask for, and what the user is actually allowed to see. Treating a scope as the whole decision is a real vulnerability.","whyWrong":["A scope says what the app may ask for, not whose photos it may read.","","Read-only is not the reason; the reason is ownership.","Ownership rules are not waived by a scope."]},{"q":"What should a resource server do when the scope claim is missing or unparseable?","options":["Retry without the token","Allow the request","Deny the request","Assume the default scope"],"answer":2,"why":"Fail closed. An absent scope claim means no permissions, never full permissions.","whyWrong":["Retrying unauthenticated is worse.","Failing open is how absent claims become privilege escalation.","","There is no safe default to assume."]},{"q":"Why is accepting an OAuth access token as proof of who the user is exploitable?","options":["They are large enough to overflow header limits, so proxies truncate them and the identity claim is lost","They expire far too quickly to be relied on, so the session breaks before the identity can be established","It authorizes an app to call an API and makes no reliable statement about the user, and may have been obtained for someone else","Access tokens are unsigned by design, so anything can forge one and claim to be any user it likes"],"answer":2,"why":"OAuth answers \"may this app do this?\" OpenID Connect adds the ID token to answer \"who is this?\"","whyWrong":["Size is irrelevant.","Expiry is unrelated.","","Many are signed; signing does not make them identity statements."]}],"idfobo":[{"q":"A gateway forwards the user original token unchanged to a downstream service. What breaks?","options":["The signature no longer validates once the gateway forwards it","The token grows past the header limit at each extra hop","Nothing, forwarding the caller's token is the documented pattern","Every service must ignore the audience check, so one token now works everywhere"],"answer":3,"why":"The aud names the first service. Accepting it downstream means abandoning audience validation, which turns the token into a universal key.","whyWrong":["The signature stays valid, which is what makes the shortcut tempting.","Forwarding copies the token unchanged. Its size never moves.","It is a common shortcut and a known anti-pattern, not a documented one.",""]},{"q":"What distinguishes delegation from impersonation in a token?","options":["Delegation records the acting party in an act claim; impersonation shows only the user","Delegation tokens are encrypted, while impersonation tokens are only signed","Impersonation tokens drop the subject claim and name the service instead","They are two names for one exchange, and the resource server picks which"],"answer":0,"why":"With an act claim the downstream can see \"service X acting for user Y\". Impersonation loses the acting party entirely.","whyWrong":["","Both are normally signed JWTs. Encryption is a separate decision.","Impersonation keeps the user in sub. That is exactly what makes it impersonation.","Token exchange treats them as distinct modes, chosen by the requester."]},{"q":"What is the may_act claim for?","options":["Setting how long the token stays valid","Listing the allowed scopes","Naming who is permitted to act for this subject","Naming the issuer that minted the token"],"answer":2,"why":"may_act gates delegation: it names which party may exchange this token to act on the subject behalf.","whyWrong":["That is exp.","That is scope.","","That is iss."]},{"q":"Why is passing X-User-Id: ada between services not an acceptable substitute for a token?","options":["It is unauthenticated, so any service that can reach the endpoint can claim to be anyone","It is slower than a token, because the receiver has to look the user up on every request","Headers are not logged by most proxies, so you lose the audit trail for the whole call","Headers are size-limited, so a user identifier gets truncated once the request grows"],"answer":0,"why":"A token is signed, so the downstream verifies the claim instead of trusting the caller. An unauthenticated header makes security depend entirely on perfect network isolation.","whyWrong":["","It is faster, and that is exactly the trap.","Logging is not the problem either.","Size is not the problem."]},{"q":"In a four-hop chain, what must be true of the token at each hop?","options":["It must be the same token throughout, so the subject cannot drift","Only the last hop needs a token, since it holds the data","Each hop mints its own unsigned token to save verification cost","Each hop needs its own audience, with the subject and actor preserved"],"answer":3,"why":"Audience-per-hop preserves the point of audience validation; the subject and act claims preserve authorization and attribution.","whyWrong":["Forwarding one token is the anti-pattern. Each hop should get its own audience.","Every hop is a checkpoint, and the middle ones are where lateral movement starts.","An unsigned token proves nothing. Verification is the whole point.",""]}],"idfactas":[{"q":"What is the core modeling mistake in a \"view as this user\" feature?","options":["Making it too slow, since each action has to be re-authorized against the impersonated account","Collapsing the two subjects, minting the customer a session instead of keeping both identities","Not encrypting the session, so the impersonated identity is visible to anything inspecting traffic","Using cookies to carry the impersonated identity, rather than a claim inside the token itself"],"answer":1,"why":"With one subject the audit log says the customer acted. Keeping the authenticated subject and the effective subject separate is what makes everything else possible.","whyWrong":["Performance is not the risk.","","Encryption does not restore attribution.","Cookies are fine as a transport."]},{"q":"What permissions should an acting-as session have?","options":["The union of both","Whatever the engineer normally has","The target permissions","The intersection of both, and usually less"],"answer":3,"why":"If acting-as adopted the target rights, acting as an admin would hand the engineer admin. Intersect, then narrow further.","whyWrong":["A union is strictly worse.","Then it would not show what the customer sees.","That is the escalation path.",""]},{"q":"Which action should stay denied in an acting-as session even when writes have been approved?","options":["Viewing configuration flags","Reading a support note","Viewing an order","Changing the account email address"],"answer":3,"why":"Changing email, changing the password, adding MFA factors, deleting the account and exporting data are all account-takeover primitives.","whyWrong":["Also a read.","Also a read.","A read, and the normal case.",""]},{"q":"Why must an acting-as session show a persistent banner?","options":["Because the specification requires a visible indicator whenever a session carries a delegation claim","To satisfy branding requirements, since the support tool must look visually distinct from the customer product","Because engineers forget which window they are in and act on production data believing it is their own account","To slow the operator down deliberately, so that destructive actions require a moment of conscious thought"],"answer":2,"why":"It is a human-factors control, and a cheap one relative to the mistake it prevents.","whyWrong":["No spec mandates it; the risk does.","Not a branding matter.","","Friction is not the goal."]},{"q":"A customer reports a rendering bug. Which option should you reach for first?","options":["Impersonate them silently, so the support work does not clutter their notification feed","Ask them for their password so you can sign in and see exactly what they are seeing","Full write acting-as, so you can reproduce the problem and fix it inside one session","A diagnostics or redacted view showing their config and screens without their content"],"answer":3,"why":"Take the lowest rung of the ladder that solves the problem, and if it fits, ask the user to screen-share, which is consent given directly.","whyWrong":["Silent impersonation destroys attribution.","Never ask for a password.","The highest-risk option for a read-only problem.",""]}],"idfzt":[{"q":"What did the perimeter model treat as authorization?","options":["A password","A signed token","Being inside the network","A certificate"],"answer":2,"why":"Authenticate once at the edge, then trust anything inside. One phished laptop puts the attacker inside, where nothing checks anything.","whyWrong":["A password was the edge check, not the interior one.","Tokens are the zero-trust answer, not the perimeter one.","","Certificates came later."]},{"q":"\"Never trust, always verify\", what does always mean here?","options":["Once per session","Every request","Once per day","Once per login"],"answer":1,"why":"Every request re-establishes who is asking and whether they may. Signed, audience-scoped, short-lived tokens are what make that cheap.","whyWrong":["Per-session is still a long window for a revoked user.","","Daily is not verification.","That is the perimeter model."]},{"q":"A request arrives from 10.0.0.5, an internal address. How much should that contribute to the access decision?","options":["It should skip authentication","It is context at most, never identity","It should be denied outright","It should be trusted as identity"],"answer":1,"why":"An IP is a routing detail that can be spoofed, reassigned or shared. Context is evidence feeding a decision, never a reason to skip one.","whyWrong":["Skipping authentication is the failure mode.","","Internal addresses are not inherently suspicious either.","That is precisely the perimeter assumption."]},{"q":"The policy decision point is unreachable. What should the enforcement point do?","options":["Permit reads only","Permit, to avoid an outage","Deny","Cache the last decision indefinitely"],"answer":2,"why":"Fail closed. A system that fails open under load is one an attacker will deliberately overload.","whyWrong":["Reads still disclose data, so this is not a safe middle ground.","Failing open to protect availability hands an attacker a way in: overload the policy engine, then walk through.","","Indefinite caching is failing open slowly, revoked access keeps working."]},{"q":"Which statement about zero trust is accurate?","options":["It is an architecture where identity, not network location, is what access decisions are made on","It means multi-factor authentication everywhere, applied uniformly to every user and service","It means trusting nothing at all, so every request is denied unless explicitly pre-approved","It is a product you can buy, sold as a gateway that enforces the model across your estate"],"answer":0,"why":"Trust is still granted constantly, it is just explicit, narrow, evidenced and short-lived rather than implied by a network cable.","whyWrong":["","MFA at the edge with a flat interior is still the perimeter model.","The name is unhelpful; verification, not absence of trust, is the point.","Vendors sell components, not the architecture."]}],"idfapikey":[{"q":"What is an API key, precisely?","options":["A signed token identifying a user","An encrypted form of the account password","A short-lived session cookie for the API","A long-lived opaque bearer token for an application"],"answer":3,"why":"Every word matters: long-lived, opaque, bearer, and it names an application rather than a person.","whyWrong":["There is no user in the picture at all.","No password goes into it; a key is generated at random.","Keys are not scoped to a session and rarely expire.",""]},{"q":"Why should an API key never travel in a URL query string?","options":["It is slower than a header, since the URL is parsed twice","Query strings travel outside the TLS-encrypted part of the request","Query strings have a length limit that a long key can exceed","URLs land in access logs, browser history and Referer headers sent onward"],"answer":3,"why":"A key in a URL has already been copied into infrastructure that assumes URLs are not secret.","whyWrong":["Parsing costs nothing, and performance is not the objection.","TLS covers the whole request line. The leak happens at the endpoints.","Length is not the issue, and keys are far shorter than any limit.",""]},{"q":"Why give every issued key an identifiable prefix such as sk_live_?","options":["It encrypts the rest of the key","It is required by the HTTP spec","Secret scanners can spot a leaked key in a public repo","It makes the key longer and harder to brute force"],"answer":2,"why":"The prefix costs nothing and turns a silent leak into an alert, and it tells an engineer at a glance what they are holding.","whyWrong":["A prefix is plain text and encrypts nothing.","No spec requires it.","","A prefix is public and adds no entropy."]},{"q":"How should API keys be stored on the server?","options":["Encrypted with a reversible key","In an environment variable","Hashed, like passwords","In plaintext, so support can read them out"],"answer":2,"why":"Show the plaintext once at creation and store only a hash, so the database never contains a usable credential.","whyWrong":["Reversible encryption means a database breach is still a credential breach.","That is where the client keeps it, not the issuer.","","If support can read it, so can an attacker with the same access."]},{"q":"Where are API keys genuinely the wrong tool?","options":["Anywhere a user is involved, or where the code runs somewhere the user can read it","A webhook receiver, where the sender needs a stable credential it can configure once","An internal script, where the caller is trusted and the blast radius is deliberately small","A nightly CI job, where no interactive user is present to complete an authorization flow"],"answer":0,"why":"A key cannot represent a user, and a key shipped in a mobile app or SPA bundle is not a secret at all.","whyWrong":["","A classic good fit.","Also a good fit.","Also a good fit, though federation is better still."]}],"idfcapurl":[{"q":"What defines a capability URL?","options":["It requires a session cookie alongside the unguessable path","The URL itself is the credential, holding the link is the authorization","It uses a dedicated HTTP method that servers treat as capability-bearing","It is defined by length, the path has to exceed a minimum size"],"answer":1,"why":"No session and no token header: possession of the unguessable link is the whole authorization.","whyWrong":["If it needed a session, the link on its own would grant nothing.","","It is an ordinary GET or POST. No special verb exists for this.","There is no length rule. What matters is that the link is the credential."]},{"q":"A password reset link is consumed before the user ever clicks it. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The token expired somewhere between sending the mail and delivery","A corporate mail security gateway fetched the link to scan it","The user clicked the link twice in quick succession","The email was encrypted and the client rewrote the link"],"answer":1,"why":"Scanners visit links to check for malware, which consumes a single-use GET. Requiring a POST to consume the token avoids it.","whyWrong":["Expiry gives a different error than a token already consumed.","","Possible, but far less common than a scanner getting there first.","Encryption does not follow links, and rewriting does not consume one."]},{"q":"Why is Referrer-Policy relevant to capability URLs?","options":["It expires the link after a fixed interval, so a leaked capability stops working on its own","It signs the URL, letting the recipient verify that the capability was issued by the expected party","It compresses the URL so that the capability token stays within the length limits proxies enforce","If the page links to or loads anything external, the full capability URL can be sent to that third party"],"answer":3,"why":"The Referer header carries the whole URL, secret included. Set no-referrer on capability pages.","whyWrong":["Expiry is a server concern.","It provides no signature.","It does no compression.",""]},{"q":"What is the trade-off between a stored capability and a signed one?","options":["Stored links cannot expire, since the record has no natural lifetime once it has been written","Stored is revocable but needs a lookup; signed is stateless but cannot be revoked before expiry","There is no meaningful difference, since both end up as an opaque string in the same URL","Signed links are always more secure, since the signature cannot be stripped or replayed by anyone"],"answer":1,"why":"It is the opaque-versus-structured token trade-off appearing again, this time in a URL.","whyWrong":["Stored links expire perfectly well.","","The revocation behavior differs sharply.","Neither is universally more secure; they fail differently."]},{"q":"What should be logged when a capability URL is used?","options":["The full URL, so that debugging has everything it might need","Only a short prefix identifying which capability was used","The user's password, to tie the use to an account","Nothing at all, since anything logged is a secret"],"answer":1,"why":"A prefix is enough to correlate the use in an audit trail without turning your logs into a credential store.","whyWrong":["That copies a working credential into your logs.","","No password is involved in a capability URL.","You still need an audit trail of who used what."]}],"idfassume":[{"q":"A role has two policies. What does the trust policy control?","options":["Who is allowed to assume the role","What the role can do once assumed","Which region it works in","How long the credential lasts"],"answer":0,"why":"The trust policy is an identity question; the permission policy is the authorization question.","whyWrong":["","That is the permission policy.","Not a policy concern.","Duration is a separate parameter."]},{"q":"A SaaS vendor is trusted by many customers. Why is an external id needed?","options":["Because being trusted by everyone means any customer could otherwise aim the vendor at another customer role","To identify the vendor, since the trust policy needs to know which principal is making the request","To speed up assumption, by letting the provider skip a lookup it would otherwise perform per call","To encrypt the request payload, so that the assumed credentials cannot be read in transit by the vendor"],"answer":0,"why":"The confused deputy: customer B names A role, and the vendor account genuinely is trusted by A, so the call succeeds. A per-customer secret breaks it.","whyWrong":["","The account id already does that.","It has no performance role.","It provides no encryption."]},{"q":"A cloud trust policy names the CI provider as OIDC issuer but places no condition on the sub claim. What is the consequence?","options":["Any repository on that CI platform can assume the role","Only private repositories can assume it","The role cannot be assumed at all","Nothing, the issuer check is sufficient"],"answer":0,"why":"The issuer check proves the token is real; the subject check proves it is the right one. You need both, exactly as with iss and aud.","whyWrong":["","Visibility is irrelevant to the trust policy.","It can be assumed, that is the problem.","The issuer vouches for every one of its tenants, not just yours."]},{"q":"What does workload identity federation remove?","options":["The audit trail, because federated calls are attributed to the platform and not the caller","The need for any authorization decision, since the workload is already inside the network","The role itself, since permissions attach directly to the workload's own identity instead","The long-lived static credential that role assumption otherwise needs to start from"],"answer":3,"why":"The workload exchanges the platform identity it already has for a role, so no secret is stored anywhere.","whyWrong":["Auditing improves, since the original identity is recorded.","Authorization still applies through the role permissions.","The role remains; only the bootstrap secret disappears.",""]},{"q":"After assuming a role, what should the audit log record?","options":["Both the original identity and the role assumed","Only the role that was assumed","Neither, role assumption is anonymous","Only the original caller who started the session"],"answer":0,"why":"Like the act claim, recording both preserves the answer to \"who did this?\" across the hop.","whyWrong":["","Then attribution to a person is lost.","Assumption is always attributable.","Then you cannot tell what permissions were used."]}],"az6":[{"q":"A role check passes and the handler returns repo.findById(id). What is the flaw?","options":["No object-level check, changing the id returns another customer record","The query is slow, since findById does a full table scan without an index","The role check runs after the record has already been loaded","The id should be a string, since numeric ids are guessable"],"answer":0,"why":"This is IDOR, or broken object-level authorization: endpoint authorization passed, ownership was never checked.","whyWrong":["","Performance is not the flaw here, and it would not leak anything.","The role check ran first and passed. Ownership is what was missed.","A random string id only hides the problem. The ownership check is still missing."]},{"q":"Does replacing sequential ids with UUIDs fix IDOR?","options":["Only for public APIs, where enumeration is the real risk","Yes, provided they are version 4 and randomly generated","No, it is obscurity, not authorization; references leak constantly","Yes, because an attacker has no realistic way to guess a valid identifier"],"answer":2,"why":"Unguessable ids stop casual enumeration, but references appear in links, logs, exports and tickets. Do both, and never let the first substitute for the second.","whyWrong":["The argument holds for internal APIs just as much.","The version changes nothing; an unguessable id is still not a check.","","Guessing is only one way to get a reference; they leak in links and logs."]},{"q":"Where should the tenant constraint live?","options":["In the query itself, so the wrong tenant simply finds nothing","In the load balancer, routing each tenant to its own pool","In a check after the fetch, comparing tenant ids in code","In the UI, by hiding rows the current tenant should not be shown"],"answer":0,"why":"Check-after fails the moment somebody adds a new query, and somebody always does. A repository that cannot fetch across tenants cannot leak across tenants.","whyWrong":["","A router picks a backend; it still cannot tell who owns record 4417.","That works until the next call site forgets to run the check.","The UI is not an authorization boundary; the API is still open."]},{"q":"Why can a gateway not enforce object-level authorization?","options":["Gateways cannot read the token, since it is encrypted end to end","It does not know who owns record 4417, only the service holding the data does","The extra lookup would be too slow at gateway request rates","Reading the request body that deep would break the TLS session"],"answer":1,"why":"Coarse checks at the edge, ownership checks next to the data. A design that pushes all authorization to the perimeter has no answer to IDOR.","whyWrong":["Gateways read and validate tokens routinely. That is much of what they are for.","","Speed is not the limit. The gateway has no way to know the answer at all.","The gateway already terminates TLS and can see the body."]},{"q":"An API returns the full entity and the UI hides the salary field. What is wrong?","options":["Nothing is wrong, since the user never sees the field the UI hides","The JSON already contains it and anyone can open developer tools","The field should be renamed to something less obvious","The response should be cached so it is fetched once"],"answer":1,"why":"Mask or omit at serialization, driven by the caller permissions. The UI is not an authorization boundary.","whyWrong":["It shows up in the network tab in seconds.","","Renaming hides nothing from anyone reading the response.","Caching spreads the data further; it does not hide it."]}],"az7":[{"q":"What is the difference between a group and a role?","options":["Groups exist only in LDAP directories, roles only in apps","A group is a collection of people; a role is a collection of permissions","They are the same thing, with the name varying by vendor","A role is bigger, since it spans several groups at once"],"answer":1,"why":"\"Who is in Finance?\" is an HR question; \"what may Finance do?\" is a security question. Collapsing them means org-chart changes become unreviewed permission changes.","whyWrong":["Groups turn up in every identity system, and roles are modeled in directories too.","","They are administered and reviewed differently, whatever the vendor calls them.","Size is not the distinction. One holds people, the other holds permissions."]},{"q":"Why must a nested-group walk track visited nodes?","options":["To sort the output, since the traversal order determines how the resulting members are listed","For performance, since revisiting a group that has already been expanded wastes directory calls","Because nothing prevents a cycle, and a naive recursive walk would never terminate","To count members accurately, since a user reachable by two paths would otherwise be counted twice"],"answer":2,"why":"Group graphs can contain cycles, so the traversal needs a visited set. It is a graph problem, and it is where hand-rolled code fails.","whyWrong":["Ordering is unrelated.","Performance is a side benefit, not the reason.","","Counting does not require it."]},{"q":"How does most accidental over-permissioning happen with groups?","options":["Someone grants a permission directly to a user, bypassing the group structure entirely","Tokens expire late, so a permission removed yesterday remains usable until the token lapses","Someone nests a group under a broader parent, silently granting its access to everyone above","Passwords are shared between colleagues, so one person's access becomes several people's access"],"answer":2,"why":"No permission was ever granted, the shape of the tree changed, and inheritance did the rest.","whyWrong":["Direct grants are visible and reviewable.","Unrelated.","","Unrelated to group structure."]},{"q":"Long-tenured employees intermittently get 431 responses after login. What is the likely cause?","options":["Clock skew between the IdP and the application servers","Token bloat, every group emitted as a claim overflows proxy header limits","Their account is locked out after too many stale sessions","Their password is long enough to overflow the login form field"],"answer":1,"why":"Users in hundreds of groups produce oversized headers. Emit only groups relevant to the audience, send ids rather than names, or look them up at the API.","whyWrong":["Skew shows up as expiry errors, not as a 431.","","A lockout produces a clear error at login, not an intermittent 431.","Passwords never reach the token, and 431 is about request header size."]},{"q":"What number should an access review report for a person?","options":["Their last login date, which shows whether any of the access is being used at all","Their effective permissions, the flattened union across all groups, nested and direct","The number of groups they belong to, as a proxy for how much access they have picked up","Their direct group memberships, which is the only thing a reviewer can actually act on"],"answer":1,"why":"If you cannot produce that on demand you cannot answer the only question an auditor will ask.","whyWrong":["Activity is a different question.","","A count says nothing about access.","Direct membership hides everything inherited."]}],"az8":[{"q":"Under deny-overrides, two policies permit and one denies. What is the result?","options":["It depends on order","Not applicable","Permit, by majority","Deny"],"answer":3,"why":"A prohibition should not be defeatable by adding another rule somewhere else. This is the safe default.","whyWrong":["Ordering matters for first-applicable, not this.","Some policies did apply.","Policy combination is not a vote.",""]},{"q":"Why is permit-overrides risky as a general setting?","options":["It is not a standardized combining algorithm","It is slower, since every rule must be evaluated","It cannot express a deny decision anywhere in the whole policy","One careless broad grant silently undoes every restriction"],"answer":3,"why":"Occasionally justified for break-glass, but as a default it means the weakest rule wins.","whyWrong":["permit-overrides is a standard combining algorithm.","Performance is identical; both algorithms visit the rules.","Deny can be expressed, it just loses to any permit.",""]},{"q":"What must happen when no policy matches a request?","options":["Log and permit","Deny","Permit","Ask the user"],"answer":1,"why":"Default deny. Default permit means every resource nobody wrote a rule for is public, and you find out from the outside.","whyWrong":["Logging does not make it safe.","","That is the failure mode.","Not a decision procedure."]},{"q":"Why keep \"not applicable\" distinct from \"deny\" inside the engine?","options":["It runs faster, since a three-valued result can be short-circuited earlier than a boolean one","The specification mandates three values, so an implementation returning two would not be conformant","It distinguishes \"a rule deliberately blocked this\" from \"no rule covered this\", very different bugs","To reduce log volume, since only genuine denials need to be recorded for later review"],"answer":2,"why":"Both collapse to denied at the enforcement point, but they need different fixes.","whyWrong":["No performance difference.","Nothing requires it; it is good practice.","","It adds detail rather than reducing it."]},{"q":"What is the most valuable output of a policy engine, beyond the decision itself?","options":["The response time","The engine version","Which policy decided","The number of policies evaluated"],"answer":2,"why":"Without it, debugging is guesswork, and the standard response to an unexplained denial is to add a broad permit until it works.","whyWrong":["Useful for ops, not for correctness.","Irrelevant to the decision.","","A count explains nothing."]}],"am4b":[{"q":"What makes a method phishing-resistant?","options":["Sending it over TLS, so an attacker positioned on the network cannot read the secret in transit","A longer code, since more digits make interception and reuse within the validity window impractical","Expiring within thirty seconds, leaving too little time for an attacker to relay it to the real site","The authenticator itself checks who is asking, because the origin is part of the cryptographic operation"],"answer":3,"why":"If the factor is something the user can read out and type, they can be induced to read it to an attacker. A code has no idea which site asked.","whyWrong":["TLS protects the wire, not the destination.","Length does not help against relay.","A proxy relays within the window.",""]},{"q":"An attacker sends repeated push approvals at 3am until the user taps to make it stop. What is the defense?","options":["Number matching, the user must type digits shown on the login screen","Switching to SMS codes, which cannot be approved by a single tap","Longer push timeouts, so a sleeping user still gets a real prompt","More frequent pushes so the user notices the pattern is unusual"],"answer":0,"why":"Number matching defeats blind approval outright, because the user must read something from the real login screen.","whyWrong":["","SMS is weaker still, and a phished code works just as well for the attacker.","A longer window gives the attacker more time, not less.","More prompts is exactly the fatigue this attack runs on."]},{"q":"A user has a passkey enrolled and also SMS as a fallback. How strong is the account?","options":["As strong as SMS","As strong as the passkey","Stronger than either alone","The average of the two"],"answer":0,"why":"The weakest enrolled method is the account real strength, because an attacker will always claim to have lost the strong one.","whyWrong":["","Only if the weak fallback is removed.","Adding a weak path never strengthens.","Security does not average."]},{"q":"Why should a biometric never be sent to a server?","options":["It is a password you can never change or revoke","A server has no way to store a raw biometric image","The template is too large to send on every login","Matching on the server would be too slow to be usable"],"answer":0,"why":"A biometric is a local gesture that unlocks a key on the device, not a factor you transmit.","whyWrong":["","They store them easily, and that is exactly the danger.","Size is not the concern. A few kilobytes travels fine.","Speed is irrelevant. The problem is what a leak costs."]},{"q":"Which is the most common route to taking over an account protected by strong MFA?","options":["Cloning the security key","Guessing the PIN","Breaking the cryptography","The account recovery flow"],"answer":3,"why":"A hardware key protecting an account whose recovery is an emailed link is protected by email.","whyWrong":["Cloning is impractical.","PINs are rate-limited by the authenticator.","Nobody breaks the cryptography.",""]}],"am4c":[{"q":"What are the two specifications that make up FIDO2?","options":["WebAuthn and CTAP2","SAML and XML-DSig","TLS and X.509","OAuth and OIDC"],"answer":0,"why":"WebAuthn is the browser API your code calls; CTAP2 is how the browser talks to an external authenticator.","whyWrong":["","A different federation stack.","Transport and certificates, not FIDO2.","Different family entirely."]},{"q":"A user signs in with Touch ID rather than a USB key. What changes on your server?","options":["It must now verify the attestation statement","It must speak CTAP2 to the platform","Nothing, both present an identical WebAuthn surface","It must store a different key type"],"answer":2,"why":"A platform authenticator involves no CTAP at all, but the server sees the same ceremony either way, and generally should not care.","whyWrong":["Attestation is optional in both cases.","CTAP is between the browser and an external device.","","The key format is the same."]},{"q":"What does a discoverable (resident) credential enable?","options":["Usernameless login, because the authenticator knows which accounts it holds","The server can verify the assertion without storing a public key","Faster signing, since the key is already unlocked in the authenticator","Longer keys, because resident storage removes the size limit"],"answer":0,"why":"Non-discoverable credentials wrap the key into the credential id, so the server must say which credentials to try, meaning the user identifies themselves first.","whyWrong":["","The relying party always keeps the public key. That is what the assertion is checked against.","Signing speed is identical. Discoverability changes storage, not the crypto.","Key length follows the algorithm and does not change with resident storage."]},{"q":"A page at login.example.com registers a credential with rpId \"com\". What happens?","options":["It works but only on that host","It silently becomes example.com","It works across all subdomains","It is rejected as a public suffix"],"answer":3,"why":"The RP ID must be the origin domain or a registrable suffix of it. A public suffix would scope the credential across an entire TLD.","whyWrong":["Rejected, not narrowed.","No silent correction occurs.","That is what example.com would do.",""]},{"q":"Why choose the RP ID carefully before registering anyone?","options":["It determines the key algorithm the authenticator will pick when it mints the credential","It affects signature speed, because a longer RP ID lengthens the data being signed","It is baked into every credential and cannot be changed without re-registering every user","It appears in the browser prompt, so a poor choice confuses users during registration"],"answer":2,"why":"Pick the broadest domain you might ever need at the start, or you will invalidate every credential later.","whyWrong":["Algorithms come from pubKeyCredParams.","No performance effect.","","Users do see the domain in the passkey prompt, but confusing wording can be fixed later and a wrong RP ID cannot."]}],"am8b":[{"q":"Why must the user handle not be an email address?","options":["Emails are too long, and the handle has a size limit that many real addresses would exceed","Emails contain characters the encoding does not permit, so the handle would fail to round-trip","The handle must be numeric, since authenticators index credentials by an integer identifier","It is stored on the authenticator and may be displayed, so it would leak to anyone holding the device"],"answer":3,"why":"The user handle should be an opaque, stable id you can key on, not a personal identifier.","whyWrong":["Length is not the issue.","Encoding is not the problem.","It need not be numeric.",""]},{"q":"What is the difference between the UP and UV flags?","options":["They are the same bit, reported twice for compatibility","UP applies to platform authenticators, UV to roaming keys","UV means the server verified the user before the assertion","UP means someone touched the device; UV means a PIN or biometric passed"],"answer":3,"why":"UP is one factor; UV makes the authenticator two factors on its own. Requesting userVerification is only a preference, you must check the bit.","whyWrong":["They are separate bits with separate meanings.","Both flags apply to either kind of authenticator.","Verification happens on the authenticator. The server only reads the bit.",""]},{"q":"What do the BE and BS flags tell you?","options":["The authenticator's battery state at the time of the assertion","Which key algorithm the authenticator picked for this credential","The signature counter value carried in the authenticator data","Whether the credential can be synced, and whether it currently is"],"answer":3,"why":"Backup eligible and backup state distinguish a synced passkey from a device-bound credential, and BE cannot change after registration.","whyWrong":["Nothing in WebAuthn reports hardware battery levels.","The algorithm lives in the COSE key, not in the flags byte.","That is signCount, a separate field with its own bytes.",""]},{"q":"Why is attestation: \"none\" a reasonable default?","options":["Omitting attestation produces a smaller response that the browser can parse and validate noticeably faster","Attestation is cryptographically unsound, so the specification recommends turning it off in production deployments","Browsers silently discard the attestation object anyway, so requesting it has no observable effect on the ceremony","Verifying it properly needs trust anchors and metadata, and phishing resistance comes from origin binding rather than knowing the brand"],"answer":3,"why":"It is genuinely useful only when an enterprise must enforce approved authenticator models. Otherwise it adds a privacy cost and no security benefit.","whyWrong":["Parsing cost is trivial.","It is secure, just usually unnecessary.","Browsers pass it through.",""]},{"q":"An origin check is written as origin.startsWith(\"https://login.example.com\"). What gets in?","options":["Only HTTP origins","Only subdomains","Nothing, it is safe","https://login.example.com.evil.com"],"answer":3,"why":"Origin validation must be exact set membership against an allow-list, never a prefix or contains comparison.","whyWrong":["Scheme is not the issue.","It admits far more than subdomains.","It is a real and common vulnerability.",""]}],"am8c":[{"q":"What does an empty allowCredentials list trigger?","options":["Usernameless login, the authenticator offers whatever discoverable credentials it holds","Registration instead of login, since an empty list signals that no credential exists yet","A fallback to password, since the browser has nothing to match against and offers the older method","An error, since the specification requires at least one credential descriptor to be present"],"answer":0,"why":"The authenticator returns a userHandle identifying who was chosen, which is why the handle must be a stable opaque id.","whyWrong":["","The ceremony type is set by the API call.","No fallback is implied.","It is a valid and deliberate request."]},{"q":"What exactly does the authenticator sign during authentication?","options":["The credential id, so the server can match the stored key","The user's password, hashed by the authenticator first","Only the challenge that the server sent for this attempt","authenticatorData concatenated with SHA-256 of clientDataJSON"],"answer":3,"why":"Because the browser writes the origin into clientDataJSON and its hash is inside the signed bytes, a phishing proxy cannot alter it.","whyWrong":["The credential id travels alongside. It is not the signed payload.","No password exists anywhere in this flow.","The challenge alone would not bind the origin to the signature.",""]},{"q":"A synced passkey reports signCount 0 and your stored value is 0. What should you do?","options":["Reject the assertion, since a counter that never advances signals a cloned key","Accept it, counters are unsupported, which is normal for synced passkeys","Force the user through registration again to reset the counter","Increment the stored value yourself so the next check passes"],"answer":1,"why":"Treating 0 as a regression locks out every synced-passkey user. A genuine regression is a signal to log and investigate, not an automatic rejection.","whyWrong":["That reasoning locks out every synced-passkey user on their first login.","","Nothing is wrong, so there is nothing to reset.","You would be inventing data the authenticator never sent."]},{"q":"You look up the stored public key by credential id and verify the signature. What else must you check?","options":["Nothing more, a valid signature settles the question","That the credential belongs to the account being logged into","The authenticator's brand, from its attestation statement","That the key length matches what you stored at registration"],"answer":1,"why":"Otherwise a credential valid for Bob authenticates Ada, an account-confusion bug.","whyWrong":["A valid signature proves the key was used, not whose account it is.","","Brand tells you nothing about who owns the account.","Length is fixed by the algorithm and cannot drift."]},{"q":"WebAuthn is very hard to attack directly. Where do realistic attacks go instead?","options":["The random challenge, which servers often reuse","The signature algorithm, since ES256 is now weak","Recovery, enrollment, downgrade and post-login session theft","The USB transport, where CTAP messages can be read off the wire"],"answer":2,"why":"The cryptography holds, so everything depends on the flows around it, and a login that was unphishable still produces an ordinary bearer cookie.","whyWrong":["A correctly generated challenge is single use and not the soft spot.","ES256 holds; the cryptography is not where the attacks land.","","CTAP is not the weak point, and reading it buys an attacker nothing."]}],"ao4b":[{"q":"Which check actually makes a token sender-constrained under DPoP?","options":["Checking that iat is recent","Checking the htu value against the request URL","Verifying the proof signature with the JWK in its own header","Comparing that JWK's thumbprint to cnf.jkt in the token"],"answer":3,"why":"Without it the proof shows only that someone owns some key. Comparing to cnf.jkt is what ties the key to this token.","whyWrong":["That limits the replay window only.","That binds the proof to the endpoint, not to the token.","Necessary but circular on its own, it proves the sender holds that key, not that the token was issued to it.",""]},{"q":"What are htm and htu for in a DPoP proof?","options":["Naming the audience, so the resource server can confirm the proof was intended for itself","Encrypting the request, since both fields feed the derivation of the transport-level key","Identifying the client, since the proof must name which application generated the key pair","Binding the proof to this HTTP method and URI, so a captured proof cannot be reused elsewhere"],"answer":3,"why":"A proof captured against one endpoint is useless against another.","whyWrong":["That is the token aud claim.","DPoP signs, it does not encrypt.","The jwk identifies the key, not the client.",""]},{"q":"Why do server-provided DPoP nonces exist when a replay cache already works?","options":["They are cheaper to compute than a full replay cache lookup, so the server avoids a round trip to shared storage","They lengthen the proof so that it carries enough entropy to resist offline guessing of the key thumbprint","A replay cache needs shared state across a fleet; a nonce lets the server choose the value with no per-request storage","They replace the proof signature entirely, so the server can validate freshness without any asymmetric cryptography"],"answer":2,"why":"Shared jti state is exactly what stateless tokens were meant to avoid, and a nonce cannot be minted in advance.","whyWrong":["Computation is not the issue.","Length is irrelevant.","","The signature is still required."]},{"q":"Why must the refresh token be constrained too?","options":["Otherwise a stolen refresh token simply mints new access tokens, making the constraint pointless","The specification forbids bearer refresh tokens everywhere, regardless of the client type","It is larger, so an unconstrained refresh token is more attractive to an attacker who finds one","Refresh tokens expire faster than access tokens, so the binding has to be re-established often"],"answer":0,"why":"Constraining access tokens while leaving refresh bearer protects nothing.","whyWrong":["","It is required for DPoP public clients specifically.","Size is irrelevant.","They live far longer."]},{"q":"When is mTLS-bound preferable to DPoP?","options":["For mobile apps, where the OS keychain holds the client certificate","In a browser app, since the certificate is selected automatically","In service-to-service and regulated environments where a PKI already exists","Whenever a CDN sits in front, since it forwards the certificate"],"answer":2,"why":"mTLS is hardware-backed and harder to steal, but needs a PKI and TLS terminated where the certificate is still visible.","whyWrong":["DPoP suits mobile far better. Provisioning a certificate per install is miserable.","Client certificates barely work in browsers, and the prompt is a usability disaster.","","Most proxies and CDNs terminate TLS and lose the client certificate entirely."]}],"oa8b":[{"q":"Why is storing an access token in localStorage discouraged?","options":["Browsers are deprecating it, and it will stop being reachable from page scripts entirely","Any JavaScript on the page can read it, so one XSS or one bad dependency exfiltrates it","It is not encrypted at rest, so anyone with disk access can read it out of the browser profile","It is too small to hold a signed token once the claim set grows past a handful of fields"],"answer":1,"why":"If your app can read the token, so can injected script. No care with the flow changes that.","whyWrong":["localStorage is not being removed.","","Encryption would not help, since the app must decrypt it to use it.","Capacity is not the issue."]},{"q":"What does a BFF actually eliminate?","options":["The need for a session, since the front end can authenticate each request independently","The durable credential in the browser, so an XSS cannot grant lasting access","Cross-site request forgery, since the cookie is no longer sent on cross-origin navigations","Cross-site scripting, since the browser no longer executes any application-supplied code"],"answer":1,"why":"Injected script can still act as the user while the page is open, but there is no token to steal and reuse from elsewhere.","whyWrong":["It creates one.","","A BFF reintroduces CSRF, because the browser now authenticates with a cookie.","XSS remains possible."]},{"q":"Adopting a BFF reintroduces which problem?","options":["Weak passwords, since the BFF is the one holding the login form","CSRF, because the browser authenticates with a cookie","Clock skew between the BFF and the token issuer","Token theft from the browser, via injected script"],"answer":1,"why":"SameSite cookies plus a per-session CSRF token on state-changing requests is the standard answer.","whyWrong":["Password strength is unaffected by where tokens are held.","","Skew is a token validation concern, not something the BFF adds.","Keeping tokens out of the browser is exactly what the BFF fixes."]},{"q":"A team genuinely cannot run any backend. What is the least-bad option?","options":["An API key baked into the bundle at build time and scoped as tightly as possible","A refresh token kept in localStorage and rotated on every use","A short-lived access token held in memory, with PKCE and no refresh token","A long-lived access token in a cookie the script can read"],"answer":2,"why":"Exposed while the page is open, but nothing durable is left behind, and re-authentication on refresh is accepted.","whyWrong":["A key in a bundle is public, and a tight scope only limits the damage.","One XSS lifts the token, and rotation just hands the thief the next one.","","A script-readable cookie is localStorage with extra steps."]},{"q":"Conceptually, what does a BFF turn a SPA back into?","options":["An identity provider, since it issues the session the browser subsequently presents","A public client, since the browser still initiates the flow and holds no durable secret","A confidential client with a session, which is what server-rendered apps always were","A resource server, since it now validates tokens rather than requesting them on its own behalf"],"answer":2,"why":"The industry spent a decade moving tokens into the browser and has spent recent years moving them back out.","whyWrong":["It does not authenticate users itself.","It stops being a public client (it now has a real secret.)","","The BFF calls the API; it is not the API."]}],"oa11":[{"q":"What is OAuth 2.1, fundamentally?","options":["A new protocol whose endpoints and messages break OAuth 2.0 clients","A consolidation of the Security BCP into the base spec, dropping discouraged grants","A new token format that replaces bearer JWTs with a binding-aware one","A replacement for OpenID Connect that folds authentication into OAuth"],"answer":1,"why":"It invents nothing. If you have been following the Security BCP you are already writing OAuth 2.1.","whyWrong":["An OAuth 2.0 client that already follows current advice works against 2.1 unchanged.","","Token formats are untouched. 2.1 says nothing new about what an access token looks like.","OIDC still sits on top and is unchanged by 2.1."]},{"q":"Which grants does OAuth 2.1 remove?","options":["Client credentials and device code","Implicit and password (ROPC)","Only implicit","Authorization code and refresh"],"answer":1,"why":"Implicit returned a token in the URL fragment; ROPC has the app collect the password. Both are superseded.","whyWrong":["Both remain.","","ROPC is removed too.","Both remain and are central."]},{"q":"What is the biggest practical change for a confidential client?","options":["PKCE becomes required, because it defends against code injection which a client secret does not address","It must stop using refresh tokens, which were removed in favor of re-authenticating the user","Nothing changes, since the requirements that were added apply only to public clients in practice","It must adopt DPoP, since bearer tokens are no longer permitted for any client type at all"],"answer":0,"why":"The old \"PKCE is for public clients\" guidance is obsolete.","whyWrong":["","Refresh tokens remain, with rotation or binding.","PKCE becomes mandatory.","Sender constraint is one option for refresh tokens, not a blanket requirement."]},{"q":"How must redirect URIs be matched?","options":["By regular expression against the path","By domain, with any path allowed","By prefix match on the registered URI","By exact string comparison, with no wildcards"],"answer":3,"why":"Loose redirect matching is one of the most reliable ways to steal an authorization code.","whyWrong":["A regex is even easier to get wrong than a prefix.","Any open redirect on that domain then becomes a code thief.","Prefix matching is the exact hole attackers use.",""]},{"q":"When auditing an existing deployment, where does the real risk usually sit?","options":["The scope names, since the profile renamed several of the values clients are expected to request","A legacy grant still enabled on the authorization server for one unmigrated client","The token format, since the profile changed what a conforming access token must contain","The key length, since the minimum acceptable size was raised for every signing algorithm"],"answer":1,"why":"The grants are removed from the specification, but servers keep supporting them for compatibility, and an enabled-but-unused grant is still enabled.","whyWrong":["Scope naming is not a 2.1 concern.","","Formats are unchanged by 2.1.","Key length is unrelated."]}],"oa2b":[{"q":"What was the original attack PKCE was designed to stop?","options":["Replay of the ID token, by binding it to a value only the original requester could produce","Authorization code interception, where another app registers the same custom URL scheme","Token expiry, by allowing a client to prove continuity without holding a long-lived secret","Password guessing, by requiring proof of the verifier before the token endpoint will respond"],"answer":1,"why":"A native app has no secret, so the authorization server could not tell the real app from a malicious one receiving the same redirect.","whyWrong":["That is what nonce addresses.","","Expiry is not an attack.","Unrelated to the code flow."]},{"q":"Why does PKCE now apply to confidential clients as well?","options":["To speed up the flow, since the verifier check replaces a slower round trip against the token endpoint","Because of code injection, where an attacker feeds the victim a code for the attacker account, a client secret does not help","Because client secrets leak from deployment configuration often enough that they can no longer be trusted alone","For consistency, so that every client type follows one flow and implementers have fewer variations to support"],"answer":1,"why":"The client is genuine; the code is foreign. The victim client holds a verifier that does not match the challenge the attacker code was bound to.","whyWrong":["It adds a hash.","","That is a different problem.","It is a real attack, not tidiness."]},{"q":"Why is it safe for the code_challenge to travel through the browser?","options":["It is short-lived, expiring with the authorization request","The browser keeps query parameters out of history and referrers","It is encrypted with the client secret before it is sent","It is a SHA-256 hash, and the verifier cannot be recovered from it"],"answer":3,"why":"The challenge is public by design; the verifier travels only on the direct back-channel POST, once, at the end.","whyWrong":["Lifetime is not what protects it. A hash is safe to publish at any age.","URLs land in history, logs and referrers. Nothing is hidden.","It is hashed, not encrypted, and a public client has no secret to use.",""]},{"q":"What is the PKCE downgrade attack?","options":["Using a shorter authorization code so the verifier can be brute-forced before it expires","Forcing the plain challenge method instead of S256 so the verifier travels in the clear","Omitting code_verifier at the token endpoint and hoping the server treats it as optional","Reusing a code challenge from an earlier authorization request that already completed"],"answer":2,"why":"If a challenge was stored, the verifier must be required. Absence is failure, not a skipped optional check.","whyWrong":["Code length is set by the server.","A related weakness, but the downgrade is the omission.","","Reuse is a different flaw."]},{"q":"How do state and code_challenge differ?","options":["They are the same protection under two names, one from OAuth and one from the PKCE extension","code_challenge replaces state entirely, so implementations should stop sending the older parameter","state binds the response to the user session (CSRF); code_challenge binds the code to the client's one-time code_verifier","state applies only to mobile clients, where the redirect can be intercepted by another app"],"answer":2,"why":"You need both, and in OIDC nonce is a third thing again, binding the ID token to this login.","whyWrong":["They defend different things.","Neither substitutes for the other.","","state applies everywhere."]}],"jose7":[{"q":"What does the signed payload of an SD-JWT actually contain for a selectively-disclosable claim?","options":["Nothing at all, the payload holds only the issuer and the expiry","The claim value in the clear, alongside a list of disclosures","The claim encrypted under a key the holder shares with the verifier","A salted digest of the claim, with the value sent separately alongside"],"answer":3,"why":"The holder can drop disclosures without breaking the signature, because the payload never changes, only which disclosures accompany it.","whyWrong":["The digests sit right there in the payload, and the verifier checks against them.","That would be an ordinary JWT, where dropping a claim breaks the signature.","SD-JWT hashes rather than encrypts, and there is no shared key.",""]},{"q":"Why does every disclosure begin with random salt?","options":["To make the disclosure longer, so that its length reveals nothing about the claim it contains","Without it a verifier could brute-force a guessable value such as a birthdate until a hash matched","To set the expiry, since the salt encodes a timestamp the verifier checks before accepting it","To identify the issuer, since the salt is derived from the issuer key and binds the two together"],"answer":1,"why":"Salt makes the digest input unguessable, and unique salts also stop a verifier correlating the same value across presentations.","whyWrong":["Length is not the goal.","","Unrelated to expiry.","The iss claim does that."]},{"q":"What problem does the key-binding JWT solve?","options":["Replay, it names this verifier and this nonce, so a captured presentation cannot be reused elsewhere or by someone else","Revocation, since the binding lets an issuer withdraw a credential that has already been handed to a holder","Selective disclosure, letting a holder reveal individual claims while keeping the remaining ones hidden","Signature verification, by carrying the issuer public key so a verifier needs no prior trust anchor"],"answer":0,"why":"The issuer puts the holder public key in cnf, and the holder signs a small JWT proving they hold the matching private key.","whyWrong":["","Revocation needs a separate status mechanism.","That is what the digests do.","The issuer signature is separate."]},{"q":"Two verifiers compare notes. Can they tell they saw the same SD-JWT credential?","options":["Only with the issuer's private key, which no verifier is ever given access to","Yes, the issuer signature is identical in every presentation, so SD-JWT is not unlinkable","No, because each presentation reveals a different subset, so there is nothing to line up","Only if the holder reused a salt across both presentations of the same credential"],"answer":1,"why":"Removing that linkage needs BBS+ or zero-knowledge proofs. SD-JWT deliberately trades unlinkability for being implementable with ordinary JOSE libraries.","whyWrong":["No key is needed to compare signatures.","","The signature is the same regardless of which disclosures are sent.","Salt reuse makes it worse, but the signature already links them."]},{"q":"A verifier receives a presentation with one disclosure. What does it learn about the withheld claims?","options":["Their values, because the issuer signs the full claim set into the payload before selection","Nothing at all, since a presentation carries only the disclosures the holder chose to send","Their names only, since the _sd array stores each withheld claim name in the clear","That they exist, because the digests are still in _sd, though not what they are"],"answer":3,"why":"An honest limitation: the verifier can see you withheld two things, which is occasionally itself informative.","whyWrong":["Digests cannot be inverted when salted.","The digests remain visible in the payload.","Names are inside the disclosure, which was not sent.",""]}],"ss6":[{"q":"Why is Single Logout structurally hard?","options":["Browsers block logout requests, since they arrive as third-party traffic in a hidden frame","One login silently created N independent app sessions, and there is no reliable way to reach them all","Logout messages are slow, so the browser times out before every relying party has been reached","The specification is incomplete, leaving the ordering of the notifications undefined between parties"],"answer":1,"why":"Independent app sessions are what make federated login fast, and they are exactly what makes logout unreliable. You cannot have one without the other.","whyWrong":["Browsers do not block it.","","Speed is not the issue.","The specs exist; distributed state is the problem."]},{"q":"A user logs out of an app and is immediately logged back in on the next click. What happened?","options":["A bug in the cookie, since it was never actually cleared","Local logout ended the app session, but the IdP session was untouched","A CSRF attack replayed the login request from another tab","The old access token never expired and was picked up again"],"answer":1,"why":"Three different things are called logout, local, IdP, and full SLO. Most complaints are a mismatch between which one the user expected.","whyWrong":["The cookie was cleared correctly. The IdP session outlives it.","","No forged request is involved. The user's own click triggers a fresh login.","A new login happened here. The old token was not reused."]},{"q":"Why is front-channel logout in terminal decline?","options":["It requires JavaScript, which some relying parties disable for the pages that handle session state","It was never standardized, so each identity provider implemented an incompatible variant of it","It is too slow, since each relying party has to be contacted in sequence before logout completes","It depends on an app cookie being sent inside a cross-site iframe, which browsers now block by default"],"answer":3,"why":"Third-party cookie restrictions remove the mechanism it relies on, and it also fails when the user closes the tab.","whyWrong":["Iframes work without scripting.","It is a published OIDC specification.","Speed is not the problem.",""]},{"q":"Why must a logout token not contain a nonce claim?","options":["It breaks the signature, because the nonce is computed after signing and cannot be covered by it","A nonce marks the JWT as an ID token, so accepting one would let an attacker submit an ID token as a logout token","It would make the token too large for the back channel, which has tighter size limits than a redirect","Nonces expire quickly, so a logout arriving late would be rejected for a reason unrelated to logout"],"answer":1,"why":"The specification forbids it precisely to keep the two token types distinguishable.","whyWrong":["The signature would still verify, which is the danger.","","Size is irrelevant.","Expiry is not the issue."]},{"q":"An app uses stateless JWT sessions. What happens when back-channel logout arrives?","options":["The JWT is rewritten in place by the identity provider, so the next request carries the revoked state with it","There is nothing to delete, back-channel logout effectively requires server-side session state or a revocation list","The session ends normally, because the token contains a logout claim the resource server checks on each request","The user is redirected to the identity provider, which notices the missing session and clears the local cookie"],"answer":1,"why":"The app must find and kill the session from sid or sub alone, which means indexing sessions somewhere.","whyWrong":["A JWT cannot be modified after issuance.","","A stateless session cannot be ended by deleting a record.","No browser is involved."]},{"q":"Given SLO is unreliable, which single measure most reduces post-logout access?","options":["More front-channel iframes, so every RP gets the logout notice","A longer logout timeout, giving slow RPs time to respond","Revoking the refresh token and the grant, plus short access token lifetimes","Clearing localStorage across every relying party origin at logout"],"answer":2,"why":"Without revoking the grant, a refresh quietly mints a new access token. Logging out does not un-issue tokens already handed out.","whyWrong":["Extra notifications do nothing about the sessions that never receive them.","Waiting longer does not shorten the life of a token already issued.","","That clears one copy. Tokens already held elsewhere keep working."]}],"ao7":[{"q":"What does a security profile such as FAPI actually do?","options":["It adds a new grant type intended for regulated sectors, alongside the ones the core specification defines","It adds new cryptography, defining algorithms that plain OAuth deployments are not permitted to use","Removes optionality, turning shoulds into MUSTs and picking one option where the base spec offered several","It replaces OAuth with a distinct protocol that happens to reuse some of the same endpoint names"],"answer":2,"why":"Every FAPI requirement is a defense already available in OAuth. The contribution is refusing to let any of them be optional.","whyWrong":["No new grants are defined.","It introduces no new primitives.","","It constrains OAuth rather than replacing it."]},{"q":"Which client authentication methods does FAPI permit?","options":["HTTP Basic only","private_key_jwt and mTLS","client_secret_basic and client_secret_post","Any, including none"],"answer":1,"why":"Asymmetric methods have no shared secret, so there is nothing that can leak from either side.","whyWrong":["Basic uses a shared secret.","","Shared secrets are exactly what is excluded.","none is never acceptable here."]},{"q":"What does PAR contribute?","options":["Longer tokens, since the request object can carry claims that would not fit in a query string safely","Encrypted tokens, by requiring the client to protect the authorization request payload end to end","Faster token issuance, because the authorization server can pre-validate parameters before the browser arrives","Request parameters go to the authorization server over the back channel, so the browser carries only a handle"],"answer":3,"why":"Parameters never appear in a URL, so they cannot be tampered with, logged or leaked through a referrer.","whyWrong":["Unrelated to token size.","PAR does not encrypt tokens.","It adds a round trip.",""]},{"q":"What distinguishes FAPI Message Signing from the baseline profile?","options":["Stronger encryption, since the profile mandates a larger key size throughout the deployment","Support for more grant types, extending the profile to flows the baseline excludes","Non-repudiation, a signed record of what each party sent, which survives the exchange","Faster flows, since signing removes a validation round trip the baseline profile requires"],"answer":2,"why":"It is a legal requirement rather than a cryptographic one, which is why it is a separate level rather than simply \"more secure\".","whyWrong":["The cryptography is comparable.","Grant support is unchanged.","","Signing adds work."]},{"q":"A deployment is fully FAPI-certified. What does that not tell you?","options":["That PKCE is in use, since the profile requires it on every authorization code flow without exception","That client authentication is strong, since the profile mandates private key JWT or mutual TLS throughout","Whether the resource server checks record ownership, protocol hardening and authorization correctness are different problems","That tokens are sender-constrained, since the profile requires binding them to the client that requested them"],"answer":2,"why":"A certified deployment can still have an IDOR on its main endpoint. Only one of these two problems has a test suite.","whyWrong":["Certification does cover that.","Certification does cover that.","","Certification does cover that."]}],"run1":[{"q":"An access token is stolen. Why can you not simply revoke it?","options":["A self-contained token verifies offline, with nothing central to switch off","Revocation endpoints are optional and most servers omit them","The token is encrypted, so the server cannot match it to a record","Only the user can revoke it, from their own consent screen"],"answer":0,"why":"Your real lever is the short lifetime, which is why 5-15 minutes is an incident-response decision rather than a performance one.","whyWrong":["","The endpoint is common enough. It just cannot recall a token already issued.","Encryption has nothing to do with whether a token can be recalled.","Revocation is a server-side action, and admins do it all the time."]},{"q":"Which compromise lets an attacker mint valid tokens with nothing in your authentication logs?","options":["A client secret","The IdP signing key","A refresh token","A session cookie"],"answer":1,"why":"No login occurs at all, the attacker forges tokens directly, so authentication logging shows nothing.","whyWrong":["Serious, but scoped to that application.","","That is one user, and revoking the grant stops it.","One user, one app."]},{"q":"During emergency key rotation, what determines your worst-case exposure window?","options":["The JWKS cache TTL at the relying parties","The number of users you have","The signing algorithm you chose","The access token lifetime"],"answer":0,"why":"You cannot force a refetch you do not control. A 24-hour cache means a 24-hour window in which forged tokens still verify.","whyWrong":["","Population size does not affect it.","Algorithm choice is unrelated.","Relevant, but the stale key is the longer window."]},{"q":"You reset a compromised user password. Why might the attacker still have access?","options":["The reset takes time to propagate to every replica, so the old password keeps working","They enrolled their own authenticator, API key, client or federated trust during the session","Passwords are cached in the application tier and only refreshed on the next restart","The reset mail was intercepted, so the attacker chose the new password before the user did"],"answer":1,"why":"Hunting persistence is the step people skip. Enumerate every credential and trust attached to the account, and everything created during the window.","whyWrong":["Propagation is near-instant.","","Not how credential storage works.","Possible but not the structural reason."]},{"q":"What should you do before rotating anything during an incident?","options":["Restart the IdP to drop any session the attacker might still be holding open","Disable MFA so responders can get into locked accounts quickly","Snapshot the logs, because identity logs often have the shortest retention","Notify the whole company before the scope is understood"],"answer":2,"why":"Rotation and restarts destroy the evidence you need to establish when the attacker got in.","whyWrong":["A restart clears the foothold and the evidence at the same time.","That hands the attacker the same shortcut. Never do it.","","Communicate, yes, but preserve the evidence first."]}],"run2":[{"q":"Why is a big-bang identity cutover rarely viable?","options":["Users dislike change, and a single disruptive event generates more support load than a gradual one","You cannot repoint hundreds of applications atomically, so every migration is a period of coexistence","It costs more, since running the old and new systems in parallel doubles the infrastructure bill","Licensing forbids running two identity providers at once, so a phased approach is contractually impossible"],"answer":1,"why":"The design question is not how to move but how to run both systems safely, often for a year.","whyWrong":["User sentiment is not the blocker.","","Cost is secondary to feasibility.","Licensing is not the constraint."]},{"q":"What is lazy password migration?","options":["Delaying the project until the old system can be decommissioned in a single planned cutover","Importing old hashes as-is, then rehashing with the new algorithm on each successful login","Forcing everyone to reset their password, so no legacy hash ever reaches the new system","Storing both hashes side by side, so either system can authenticate the user during the overlap"],"answer":1,"why":"Users notice nothing and the population migrates itself; after a cutoff you force a reset for whoever is left, usually inactive accounts.","whyWrong":["Not a scheduling term.","","That is the option of last resort.","Nested hashing is a different technique."]},{"q":"Why can passkeys generally not be migrated to a new IdP?","options":["They are bound to the RP ID, and a different RP ID means every user re-enrolls","They expire after a fixed period and must be created again","The credential format is proprietary to each authenticator vendor","The private key is stored server-side and cannot be exported"],"answer":0,"why":"The RP ID is baked into the credential at registration and cannot be changed. Plan for re-enrollment, and use it to enroll something stronger.","whyWrong":["","Passkeys carry no expiry. They live until somebody deletes them.","WebAuthn is an open standard, and the formats are published.","The private key never leaves the device. The server keeps only the public half."]},{"q":"Users log in successfully after a migration but see empty accounts. What went wrong?","options":["The password migration, since the hashes were rewritten but the attribute mapping was not","Identifier mapping, apps stored the old identifier and the new IdP issues a different sub","MFA enrollment, since the second factor was re-registered against a different user record","TLS configuration, since the new endpoint presents a certificate the client silently rejects"],"answer":1,"why":"Build the mapping before cutover and assert the legacy identifier as an extra claim during the overlap. Never join on email.","whyWrong":["Authentication clearly worked.","","MFA is unrelated to account contents.","Unrelated."]},{"q":"Which application should you migrate first?","options":["The most important one, so the work gets executive attention and resourcing from the start","A low-traffic internal app, so the first real cutover teaches you what the runbook got wrong","The largest one, because getting the hardest integration done first de-risks everything after it","All of them together, so you only have to run the old and the new stack side by side once"],"answer":1,"why":"Migrate in order of recoverability, not importance. The crown jewels go last, when the process is boring.","whyWrong":["That maximizes the cost of learning.","","Size is not the criterion.","That is a big bang."]}],"run3":[{"q":"The IdP is down. What typically still works?","options":["Only administrative access, because break-glass accounts are designed to bypass the IdP","Existing sessions, until they expire, so an outage looks small at first and grows","New logins keep working but refreshes fail, since only refresh talks to the IdP directly","Nothing, every request is checked against the IdP so the whole estate stops at the same moment"],"answer":1,"why":"That shape is why session lifetime is a continuity parameter as well as a security one.","whyWrong":["Admins are affected like everyone else.","","New logins are exactly what fails.","Sessions already established are unaffected."]},{"q":"A verifier cannot reach the JWKS endpoint. What should it do?","options":["Serve stale cached keys and keep verifying","Retry the fetch until it succeeds","Accept all tokens until keys return","Reject every token until keys return"],"answer":0,"why":"A verifier that hard-fails on an unreachable JWKS turns an IdP blip into a total outage of every API.","whyWrong":["","Retrying does not answer the current request.","An unverified token is not evidence of anything.","That is the amplification to avoid."]},{"q":"What is the recursive failure that makes break-glass accounts useless?","options":["They expire quietly, and nobody notices until the credential is needed and has already lapsed","Nobody knows they exist, since the people who created them have usually moved on by the time they matter","Their credentials live behind the thing they exist to bypass, a password manager or wiki behind SSO","They are too powerful, so the controls placed around them make routine testing prohibitively risky"],"answer":2,"why":"Also check where the alert goes: a chat tool behind SSO cannot be read during the incident.","whyWrong":["Expiry is a real problem, but not the recursive one.","A documentation issue, not a structural one.","","Strength is not a failure mode."]},{"q":"How often should break-glass accounts be tested?","options":["Never, since exercising the account creates exactly the exposure the controls around it exist to prevent","Once at creation, to confirm the credential works, after which the sealed record should not be disturbed","Regularly, an untested one has usually expired, been disabled by a cleanup script, or lost its password","Only after an incident, when the procedure is fresh and the gaps it revealed are still well understood"],"answer":2,"why":"An untested emergency procedure is a hope, not a control.","whyWrong":["Untested means unavailable when needed.","Estates change around it.","","That is too late."]},{"q":"What is the tension between short sessions and continuity?","options":["There is no real tension, both goals point the same way","Long sessions are always safer, since users reauthenticate less","Short sessions cost more, each renewal hits the IdP again","Short sessions limit compromise but degrade faster during an outage"],"answer":3,"why":"Name the trade-off rather than pretending it does not exist; it is a deliberate choice with two legitimate answers.","whyWrong":["They pull against each other, which is why it is a choice.","Fewer prompts also means a stolen session lasts far longer.","The extra calls are cheap. Cost is not what drives this decision.",""]}],"run4":[{"q":"Which identity metric should you instrument first?","options":["CPU and memory usage across the nodes running your IdP cluster","The number of groups defined across the directory","The average size of the access tokens you issue","Time to deprovision, leaver event to access actually gone"],"answer":3,"why":"It is the number audits actually fail on, and it is usually far worse than anyone assumes. Measure it before claiming it.","whyWrong":["Infrastructure health says nothing about identity outcomes.","A count is not an outcome, and it never improves on its own.","Token size is a transport detail, unrelated to governance.",""]},{"q":"Which single log field most improves your ability to investigate a login failure?","options":["A correlation id spanning the redirect chain","The hostname of the server that answered","The user agent string of the browser","The size of the response body in bytes"],"answer":0,"why":"An OIDC login crosses the app, the IdP and back. Without a shared id you cannot follow one user through three systems.","whyWrong":["","Useful, but not the linking field.","Occasionally useful, rarely decisive.","Not useful here."]},{"q":"A middleware dumps request headers on error. Why is that dangerous?","options":["Bearer tokens accumulate in a log platform that usually has broader access than production","It is slow, since serializing every header on the error path adds latency exactly when load is high","Headers are encrypted at the transport layer, so what gets logged is unreadable anyway","It breaks tracing, because the dumped headers duplicate the span context the collector expects"],"answer":0,"why":"Redact at the logging layer rather than each call site, so it cannot be forgotten.","whyWrong":["","Performance is not the issue.","Headers are plaintext at the application.","Tracing still works."]},{"q":"Why is \"login failed\" an inadequate log line?","options":["It lacks a timestamp, so events cannot be put in order later","It is logged at info when a failure should be a warning","It omits the reason, the one field that makes the failure reconstructable","It is too short for a log aggregator to index it usefully"],"answer":2,"why":"Origin mismatch, expired token and unknown user are entirely different incidents that produce the same generic message.","whyWrong":["Timestamps come from the logger, and ordering is not what is missing here.","Severity is a detail. A warning with no reason is just as useless.","","Length is not the problem. A long line with no reason helps nobody."]},{"q":"Why is identity log retention a security decision?","options":["Writing identity events synchronously slows the IdP, so shorter retention keeps authentication latency down","Regulation fixes the window at thirty days, so the decision is a compliance question rather than a security one","These logs are often the shortest-retained and the first needed, so a short window prevents establishing when an attacker got in","Storage is the binding constraint, so retention is chosen by whatever the logging budget will comfortably absorb"],"answer":2,"why":"An investigation that can only see seven days cannot answer the question that matters.","whyWrong":["Retention does not affect performance.","Requirements vary and are rarely the binding factor.","","Cost is a constraint, not the reason."]}],"run5":[{"q":"Where do most valuable identity tests belong?","options":["Load tests, since identity checks run on every single request","Unit level, token validation and authorization logic need no network","Manual testing, because login flows involve a real browser","End to end against a real IdP, so nothing important is mocked away"],"answer":1,"why":"E2E tests are slow and flaky and prove only that the wiring is connected; the bugs that matter are in validation logic a unit test catches in milliseconds.","whyWrong":["Throughput is a separate concern from whether the check is correct.","","A manual pass is not repeatable and it never runs in CI.","E2E proves the wiring is connected, and it is slow and flaky for logic."]},{"q":"Which test case do teams almost never write?","options":["A token that expired one second ago","The happy path with a valid session","A record belonging to another tenant","A wrong password on the login form"],"answer":2,"why":"Broken object-level authorization tops the OWASP API risks and produces no error when it is missing.","whyWrong":["Expiry tests are standard and almost always present.","That is the first test anyone writes.","","Login failure tests are standard in every suite."]},{"q":"Why sign test tokens with your own generated key pair?","options":["Real identity providers forbid automated testing, so a local key is the only permitted route","It avoids licensing cost, since test tokens issued by the real provider count against the seat quota","It lets you produce expired, malformed and wrongly-audienced tokens on demand and deterministically","It is faster, since a local key avoids a network round trip on every token the test suite mints"],"answer":2,"why":"A real IdP will not issue you a broken token, which is exactly what the negative tests need.","whyWrong":["They do not.","Not the reason.","","Speed is a side benefit."]},{"q":"What is wrong with an \"auth off in dev\" switch?","options":["It breaks the build once the production profile is compiled in","It has a habit of shipping, so the path you test is not the path you run","It is hard to implement without a second copy of the login code","Nothing, since the flag is compiled out of production builds by default"],"answer":1,"why":"Use a mock IdP instead, so the real code path is exercised everywhere.","whyWrong":["The build is happy. That is why the switch survives all the way to release.","","It takes one if statement, which is precisely the problem.","No default compiles it out. Somebody has to remember, and eventually nobody does."]}],"run6":[{"q":"What is the first thing to establish when diagnosing a login failure?","options":["The user password age","Which browser the user has","The last step of the flow that worked","The server load"],"answer":2,"why":"The failure is almost never where the error appears. Locating it in the chain eliminates most hypotheses at once.","whyWrong":["Almost never relevant.","Occasionally relevant, rarely the cause.","","Unrelated."]},{"q":"A user reports a 403 immediately after logging in successfully. Where do you look?","options":["Authorization, they authenticated fine and then could not see something","The MFA enrollment, since a missing factor blocks the request","The password policy, which may have expired their credential","The IdP configuration, most likely the redirect URI list"],"answer":0,"why":"The most commonly misdiagnosed identity symptom. Authentication and authorization have different owners and different fixes.","whyWrong":["","A missing factor stops the login itself, and this login succeeded.","An expired password fails at sign-in, and the sign-in worked.","Authentication demonstrably worked, so the redirect was fine."]},{"q":"The app and IdP redirect back and forth in a loop. What is the usual cause?","options":["A slow network, where the redirect times out before the session cookie has been written","A wrong client secret, so the token exchange fails and the app restarts the flow each time","The app cannot set or read its cookie: SameSite, Secure over http, or a domain mismatch","An expired token, which the app keeps presenting and the provider keeps refusing to accept"],"answer":2,"why":"The app never sees its own session, so it starts the flow again, forever.","whyWrong":["Latency does not loop.","That fails at the token endpoint instead.","","Expiry produces an error, not a loop."]},{"q":"Nothing changed and it broke anyway. What class of bug is this?","options":["An expiry, a certificate, client secret, metadata signing cert or CRL reached its date","A DNS failure, where a record that had resolved for months stopped resolving without warning","A memory leak, which had been accumulating quietly until the process finally crossed its limit","A race condition, where two code paths that always overlapped happened to interleave differently"],"answer":0,"why":"These fail suddenly and completely at whatever hour they were issued years earlier. Alert on expiry dates well in advance.","whyWrong":["","Possible, but expiry is the classic identity case.","Leaks degrade gradually.","Races are usually intermittent, not total."]},{"q":"Only one user is affected. What does that point to?","options":["Their data, account state, group membership, enrolled factors","A signing certificate that expired overnight","A key rotation that left one side with a stale JWKS","The IdP being down, or otherwise refusing to issue new sessions"],"answer":0,"why":"Everyone affected points at configuration or keys; one user points at that user data.","whyWrong":["","An expired certificate breaks every login at once.","A stale key set fails for everyone, not for one person.","An outage takes the whole user base with it."]}],"run7":[{"q":"You are paged and it is unclear whether this is an outage or an attack. What posture do you take?","options":["Wait for more data","Assume outage and restore fast","Assume attack for the first ten minutes","Escalate and stop"],"answer":2,"why":"That stance is cheap to relax, and evidence is impossible to un-destroy.","whyWrong":["Waiting is itself a decision, and a poor one.","Restoring carelessly can destroy evidence or hand access straight back.","","Escalation does not replace containment."]},{"q":"Which signal most suggests an attack rather than an outage?","options":["Errors that start at the moment a new build rolls out","New clients, keys, factors or federated trusts appearing","A raised error rate across the whole authentication path","A uniform failure that hits every user in every region"],"answer":1,"why":"Outages do not create artifacts. Successes where there should be failures are the other tell.","whyWrong":["A deploy correlation suggests an outage.","","A normal error rate with unusual activity is the worrying case.","Uniformity suggests an outage."]},{"q":"Why is a rolling restart risky during a suspected attack?","options":["It takes too long, and the window while nodes cycle is exactly when the service is least available","It triggers alerts across monitoring, burying the signals that the responders actually need to see","It invalidates issued certificates, so every client has to re-establish trust before it can connect","It clears in-memory session state and the attacker foothold together with your ability to see what they did"],"answer":3,"why":"Contain and preserve evidence before restoring.","whyWrong":["Duration is not the concern.","Alerts are wanted.","Restarts do not affect certificates.",""]},{"q":"What single communication most reduces the load on a responder?","options":["A clear apology to affected customers, which cuts the volume of inbound complaints","Saying what is broken AND what still works, such as \"existing sessions are unaffected\"","A detailed root-cause analysis posted early, so nobody has to ask what actually went wrong","A status page color change, which signals severity without anyone having to write prose"],"answer":1,"why":"It is genuinely useful to thousands of people and stops the support flood that otherwise consumes the responder.","whyWrong":["Sentiment does not inform anyone.","","Far too early, and not what people need.","Too little information."]},{"q":"An incident is resolved. What makes the review worthwhile?","options":["Identifying who made the change, so the lesson lands with them","A written apology sent to every customer the outage touched","Adding a manual check to the release checklist for next time","Concrete changes, an earlier alert, a capped cache TTL, a runbook not behind SSO"],"answer":3,"why":"An incident that produces no change to the system will happen again. \"Be more careful\" is not an action.","whyWrong":["Blameless reviews look for the conditions that let the change ship, not for a person.","An apology may be owed, but it changes nothing about the next outage.","Manual checks decay. Within a month nobody reads that line.",""]}],"run8":[{"q":"Which identity change cannot be undone by reverting configuration?","options":["Changing the RP ID, every passkey registered under it is permanently unusable","Enabling MFA enforcement across every user in the tenant","Rotating the signing key every relying party validates against","Adding a new client registration with its own redirect URIs"],"answer":0,"why":"Ask of any change: what will exist afterwards that did not before, and what happens to it if I revert?","whyWrong":["","Turn it off again and the enrollments already completed do no harm.","Publish the new key alongside the old and there is a window to revert into.","A client can be deleted, and nothing durable is left behind."]},{"q":"What makes a rollout revertible?","options":["Accepting the new value alongside the old, and removing the old only once the new is proven","A feature flag, so the new path can be switched off without redeploying the previous build","A maintenance window, so any rollback happens while nobody is depending on the service","A canary, so a small share of traffic meets the change before the rest of the fleet does"],"answer":0,"why":"Two phases with a gap. Removing the old thing immediately is the common mistake that makes revert impossible.","whyWrong":["","Useful, but the additive phase is what creates the escape route.","A window does not make a change reversible.","Canaries limit exposure without creating a revert path."]},{"q":"Why are authorization changes more dangerous than authentication changes?","options":["They are harder to write than an authentication rule is","They need a longer test cycle before they can safely ship","An over-permissive policy fails silently, with nobody complaining","They affect more systems than an authentication change does"],"answer":2,"why":"A policy that denies too much generates tickets within the hour; one that permits too much generates nothing at all.","whyWrong":["Difficulty is not the issue here. Silence is.","Test time is a schedule question, not what makes the risk different.","","Not necessarily. An authentication change can touch every login at once."]},{"q":"How should you validate an authorization change before rollout?","options":["Ask the policy author what they intended, since they know which cases the change is meant to cover","Deploy to staging and watch for errors, since real traffic exposes what review would miss","Diff the effective permissions before and after, and test the negative cases explicitly","Review the policy text carefully, since the wording is what will be evaluated at runtime"],"answer":2,"why":"Policy text can look correct and compose into something far broader once other rules apply.","whyWrong":["Author review is not verification.","Staging rarely has realistic permission data.","","Text review misses interactions."]},{"q":"If the IdP is unavailable for four hours, what still works?","options":["Existing sessions, if their lifetime exceeds the outage","Only admin tooling, which keeps a local login","Only read operations, since every write needs a freshly issued token","Nothing, every request revalidates with the IdP"],"answer":0,"why":"That is the whole answer, which makes session lifetime a continuity parameter as much as a security one.","whyWrong":["","Admin tooling usually sits behind the IdP too.","The distinction is session age, not operation type.","Established sessions keep working without revalidating."]}],"run9":[{"q":"Why do feature comparisons rarely discriminate between identity vendors?","options":["Vendors hide the features that would separate them from rivals","Every serious product does OIDC, SAML, SCIM and MFA, so everyone scores well","Buyers lack the expertise to read a feature grid properly","Features change too fast for any comparison to stay current"],"answer":1,"why":"Constraints discriminate. Start from your own estate, not from the market.","whyWrong":["Feature lists are published in detail. Hiding is not the problem.","","Even an expert gets nothing from a grid where everyone ticks every box.","Churn is real, but the grid would be useless even if it were frozen."]},{"q":"Which single question eliminates most of the market immediately?","options":["Who are the users, workforce, customers, partners or machines","What the platform costs per monthly active user","Which cloud the platform runs in, and in which region","How many prebuilt integrations the vendor's catalog lists today"],"answer":0,"why":"Workforce IAM optimizes for governance and lifecycle; CIAM for conversion, scale and privacy. Using one for the other is a common and expensive mistake.","whyWrong":["","Cost matters, but it does not narrow the field.","Rarely decisive, and most vendors cover the same ground.","Integration counts are marketing, not a selection criterion."]},{"q":"Which part of your application estate determines the project?","options":["The modern OIDC majority, since it carries most of the user traffic","The newest applications, because they set the pattern everything else copies","The awkward tail, header-based auth, Kerberos, direct LDAP binds, hardcoded passwords","The single largest application, since its user count dominates the rollout"],"answer":2,"why":"Ask every vendor how they handle your three worst applications, by name.","whyWrong":["Those apps integrate in an afternoon each and barely move the timeline.","New apps are the easy case, and the old ones will not be rewritten to match them.","","Size and difficulty are different things. A big modern app is still a simple migration."]},{"q":"Why should a gate never be folded into a weighted score?","options":["Scores are more accurate, since they weigh everything at once","Gates are too subjective to survive a weighted comparison","A weighted total will hide a dealbreaker behind a strong showing elsewhere","It complicates the arithmetic once the weights are normalized"],"answer":2,"why":"Fail a gate and the option is out, with no score at all, that is what makes the evaluation honest.","whyWrong":["A score is not more accurate than a constraint. It only blurs it.","Gates should be the most objective criteria you have. That is why they are gates.","","The arithmetic is trivial. The problem is what the total conceals."]},{"q":"What is usually the largest and most underestimated cost of an identity decision?","options":["Migration and ongoing operation","Hardware for the identity servers","The license fee per seat","Training the support team"],"answer":0,"why":"Weight migration effort and operational burden most heavily; license price and developer experience dominate most evaluations for no good reason.","whyWrong":["","Usually irrelevant now.","The most visible cost, rarely the largest.","A minor line item."]},{"q":"What should a recommendation to a decision-maker include?","options":["A single winner and nothing else, so the decision-maker is not slowed down by alternatives they did not ask for","Only the technical analysis, leaving the choice itself to the person with the authority to make it","The vendor proposals in full, so the decision-maker can weigh the original material rather than your summary","Two or three viable options with trade-offs, total cost, risks, reversibility, and what happens if you do nothing"],"answer":3,"why":"Decision-makers want a defensible choice they can own. \"What happens if we do nothing\" is often the strongest argument and the most commonly omitted.","whyWrong":["That asks them to rubber-stamp rather than decide.","Cost and risk are the parts they own.","Raw proposals are not analysis.",""]}],"ei1b":[{"q":"What is the security boundary in Active Directory?","options":["The domain","The organizational unit","The site","The forest"],"answer":3,"why":"Compromise anywhere in a forest is compromise everywhere. Splitting into domains gives administrative separation and almost no security separation.","whyWrong":["A replication and administration boundary, however much the org chart wishes otherwise.","Where policy is applied and administration delegated.","A network topology concept.",""]},{"q":"Domain A trusts domain B. Who can access whose resources?","options":["Neither until configured further","B users can access A resources","A users can access B resources","Both directions"],"answer":1,"why":"Trust direction is the opposite of access direction, which is why people routinely configure it backwards.","whyWrong":["The trust itself enables it.","","That is the intuition, and it is inverted.","Only with a two-way trust."]},{"q":"What does DCSync abuse consist of?","options":["Using replication rights to ask a DC for every password hash, a legitimate protocol operation","Malware installed on a domain controller, which then exfiltrates the directory database offline","Stealing a backup tape containing the directory database and extracting the hashes from it","Brute-forcing the KDC until a service ticket is issued for an account the attacker does not hold"],"answer":0,"why":"No malware and no access to the DC is required, which is why replication-rights grants deserve their own alert.","whyWrong":["","No code needs to run on the DC.","That is NTDS.dit theft, a different path.","Nothing is brute-forced."]},{"q":"Why is a Domain Admin logging into a workstation dangerous?","options":["Workstations do not write logon events into the security log","The logon is slow, since group policy reapplies on every login","Their credential material then exists on the lowest-tier machine in the estate","It violates the client access licensing on that workstation"],"answer":2,"why":"Credentials never flow downward. This single discipline breaks most real attack paths.","whyWrong":["Workstations audit logons just as servers do.","Speed has nothing to do with why this breaks tiering.","","Licensing does not enter into it. The problem is where the credential ends up."]},{"q":"Why do tools that map AD attack paths exist?","options":["No single permission looks alarming, but a chain of them reaches Domain Admin, auditing one at a time never finds it","Active Directory has no real permission model, so tools have to infer one from observed behavior","Directory permissions are undocumented by Microsoft, so the only way to learn them is to probe a live domain","Microsoft requires such tooling for compliance attestation, so every audited environment runs one"],"answer":0,"why":"AD is a graph and attackers think in paths. Defenders should run the same analysis first.","whyWrong":["","It has a very detailed one.","Permissions are visible; the problem is composition.","They are third-party tools."]},{"q":"In a hybrid deployment, what tier is the directory-sync server?","options":["Tier 0, it holds credential material for the whole directory and can write to both sides","Tier 1, since it manages servers and workstations but never the directory itself","Tier 2, it is an application server that happens to move records between two directories","It depends on the sync model, and password hash sync makes it a tier 2 system"],"answer":0,"why":"It is frequently treated as an ordinary server, which is one of the most common serious misconfigurations in hybrid estates.","whyWrong":["","Still too low.","That is the mistake.","Every model gives it enormous reach."]}],"ei2b":[{"q":"A Kerberos service ticket is encrypted with which key?","options":["The KDC public key","The krbtgt key","The service account key","The user key"],"answer":2,"why":"That single fact is the whole of Kerberoasting: request tickets for every SPN and crack them offline with no failed logins.","whyWrong":["Kerberos is symmetric here.","That encrypts the TGT.","","The user key protects pre-authentication."]},{"q":"Why can any authenticated user request a service ticket for any SPN?","options":["It is a misconfiguration in most domains, and correcting it removes the exposure entirely","It requires group membership, so a user outside the service's group is refused by the KDC","It is how the protocol distributes tickets, authorization happens at the service, not the KDC","Only administrators can, since requesting a ticket for a service requires elevated privilege"],"answer":2,"why":"Not a bug, but it means service account password strength is the real control. gMSA with machine-generated passwords removes the problem.","whyWrong":["It is default protocol behavior.","No membership is required.","","Any authenticated principal can."]},{"q":"What does stealing the krbtgt key allow?","options":["Resetting the password on any account in the domain, including admins","Forging a TGT for any user with any group SIDs in the PAC","Decrypting TLS traffic to the domain controller","Reading every mailbox on the Exchange server"],"answer":1,"why":"The golden ticket. This is why krbtgt compromise means rebuilding or rotating, not resetting admin passwords.","whyWrong":["Forgery is stronger than a reset, and nobody sees a reset happen.","","The krbtgt key has nothing to do with TLS session keys.","Not directly; you would forge a ticket first and go from there."]},{"q":"Why must the krbtgt key be rotated twice?","options":["It is a Microsoft bug that has never been properly fixed","So the new key replicates out to every domain controller in turn","The KDC accepts the previous key, so one rotation leaves forged tickets working","For redundancy, so a spare key is on hand if the first is lost"],"answer":2,"why":"One rotation feels like remediation and achieves nothing.","whyWrong":["Keeping the previous key is deliberate, so tickets survive a rotation.","Replication runs on its own schedule and does not need a second rotation.","","There is no spare key here. The second rotation retires the old one."]},{"q":"What makes a silver ticket stealthier than a golden ticket?","options":["It never expires, because no ticket lifetime is embedded in it","The KDC is never contacted, so there is no ticket request to log","It is encrypted with a stronger cipher than a golden ticket","It is smaller, so it slips under packet inspection rules"],"answer":1,"why":"A service validates a ticket entirely offline with its own key, so forging one leaves no trace at the KDC.","whyWrong":["It carries a lifetime and expires like any other ticket.","","Both use the same encryption. Only the key differs.","Size is irrelevant, and nothing inspects ticket length."]},{"q":"Why is unconstrained delegation so dangerous?","options":["It is slow, because every request forces an additional round trip to the domain controller","The user entire TGT is cached on the service, so any admin there can become that user anywhere","It relies on RC4 for ticket encryption, so the material can be cracked offline given enough samples","It bypasses multi-factor authentication, since the forwarded ticket carries no record of how it was obtained"],"answer":1,"why":"Worse still, coercing a domain controller to authenticate to such a host yields the DC TGT. Treat any such host as Tier 0.","whyWrong":["Performance is not the issue.","","Encryption type is a separate concern.","MFA is not part of the Kerberos exchange."]},{"q":"Why is resource-based constrained delegation a common escalation path?","options":["It has no time limit, so a delegation configured once remains usable indefinitely without renewal","It is enabled by default on every domain controller, so most environments are exposed without configuring anything","It leaves no trace in the security event log, so defenders have no way to detect that it was used","The attribute lives on the target computer object, so anyone who can write to it can grant themselves delegation"],"answer":3,"why":"A permissions problem rather than a protocol flaw, which is why write access to computer objects deserves auditing.","whyWrong":["Tickets still expire.","It must be configured.","It is auditable.",""]}],"s2s9":[{"q":"What assumption do AI agents break that every protocol so far relied on?","options":["That a human is present when authorization happens","That tokens expire on a human timescale","That TLS is available on every hop","That every scope maps to one user action"],"answer":0,"why":"Consent screens, step-up prompts and re-authentication all assume someone is there to respond. An agent working at 2am is not.","whyWrong":["","Expiry works the same for an agent, so it is not what breaks.","Transport security is unchanged by agents.","Scopes were never that granular, and agents did not change that."]},{"q":"How many identities should an agent call carry?","options":["One, the user, since the agent acts on their behalf","One, the agent, since it is the party actually making the call","Two, the user as subject, and the agent as the acting party","Three, adding the tool being called as a third party"],"answer":2,"why":"Presenting only the user identity is impersonation, and nothing downstream can tell that software rather than a person decided.","whyWrong":["That hides the fact that software, not a person, made the call.","That loses whose data is at stake downstream.","","The tool is the audience for the call, not an identity in the chain."]},{"q":"A sub-agent requests a scope its parent was never granted. What should happen?","options":["Deny, narrowing must be monotonic","Allow it if the user consented once","Allow, sub-agents are trusted","Escalate to the user"],"answer":0,"why":"If any hop can request more than it was given, the whole chain is only as strong as its most compromised link.","whyWrong":["","Consent was for the original grant, not an expansion.","Trust does not travel upward in privilege.","The correct answer is to refuse, not to ask."]},{"q":"Why is prompt injection an authorization problem rather than a model problem?","options":["Models cannot be improved past a point, so the residual failure rate has to be handled elsewhere","Prompts are unauthenticated, so any caller can submit one and the model has no way to verify the sender","Hostile content can only cause actions the agent token already permits, so the fix is not granting the authority","It is a network issue, since the injected content arrives over a channel nobody is inspecting"],"answer":2,"why":"The token is the control, not the prompt. This is the most important consequence of agents for identity work.","whyWrong":["Models improve, and it does not close this.","True but not the mechanism that limits damage.","","Nothing to do with the network."]},{"q":"Which action should return to a human even when the agent is authorized?","options":["Sending money","Listing records","Reading a document","Searching an index"],"answer":0,"why":"Reads and reversible writes proceed; irreversible or externally-visible actions escalate. Design the interrupt before it is needed.","whyWrong":["","Reversible and low risk.","Reversible and low risk.","Reversible and low risk."]}],"az9":[{"q":"In Zanzibar, how are permissions stored?","options":["They are not stored, they are derived by walking relation tuples","In an access control matrix of every subject against every object","As named roles attached to each user account record","As a flat permission list stored against each user"],"answer":0,"why":"Everything is a subject-relation-object tuple, and a check is a graph traversal. That is what makes the model expressive and every check a traversal.","whyWrong":["","A matrix cannot express nesting or inherited relations.","Roles are one thing you can model with tuples, not the storage.","No such list exists; a check is a traversal over tuples."]},{"q":"What is the \"new enemy\" problem?","options":["A caching bug in clients, where a stale decision is reused after the underlying policy has changed","A denial-of-service attack in which repeated permission checks exhaust the authorization service","A newly emerged class of attacker that existing authorization models were never designed to handle","A stale replica applies a later write without an earlier one, so a removed user sees newly added content"],"answer":3,"why":"Each write was correct; the order was lost. This is why eventual consistency is unacceptable for authorization.","whyWrong":["It is a distributed-systems ordering problem, not a client bug.","Availability is a separate concern.","Not about attacker taxonomy.",""]},{"q":"What does a zookie do?","options":["Identifies the user, carrying the subject identifier the authorization service evaluates against","Signs the request, so the authorization service can confirm which caller asked the question","Caches the decision, so a repeated check for the same subject and object avoids a round trip","Tells the system to evaluate against a snapshot at least as recent as an observed write"],"answer":3,"why":"The client carries a token forward rather than needing a global clock, and the system guarantees it will not answer from an older state.","whyWrong":["It carries no identity.","It is not a signature.","It constrains freshness rather than caching.",""]},{"q":"What does a central authorization service cost you?","options":["Compatibility with OAuth, which it cannot be made to sit alongside","A hard runtime dependency on the critical path of every request","Nothing, the consistency it buys has no matching cost","Storage only, since the tuples grow without bound"],"answer":1,"why":"Weigh it honestly, and decide deliberately what happens when the service is unreachable, fail closed and an authorization outage is a total outage.","whyWrong":["It composes with OAuth without trouble.","","There is a real trade: every check now crosses the network.","Storage is the minor cost next to the availability coupling."]},{"q":"When do you probably NOT need a relationship graph?","options":["When your data is a tree of deeply nested folders to protect","When many different services must agree on the same authorization answer","When users share documents arbitrarily with one another","When roles map cleanly to permissions and authorization is per-endpoint"],"answer":3,"why":"Most applications do not need Zanzibar; a tenant-scoped query with an ownership check is the right answer.","whyWrong":["Nesting is the case a relationship graph exists for.","Shared answers are a reason to centralize, not to skip it.","That is exactly when you do need the graph.",""]}],"oa12":[{"q":"What problem does OpenID Federation solve?","options":["Bilateral trust scaling quadratically, everyone configuring everyone","Signatures that are too weak for a large ecosystem to rely on","Logins that are slow because metadata is fetched at each one","Tokens expiring before a federated login finishes its round trip"],"answer":0,"why":"Ten parties need forty-five relationships. Ecosystems with thousands of participants need trust proven on demand instead.","whyWrong":["","Signature algorithms are unchanged. Federation is about who to trust, not how to sign.","Chain resolution adds work rather than removing it. Caching is what keeps it bearable.","Expiry is unrelated. Federation settles trust, not lifetimes."]},{"q":"How does a relying party trust an OP it has never been configured with?","options":["Through a shared secret distributed by the federation operator","By checking that the OP's issuer URL resolves in public DNS","It trusts any OP that publishes a valid entity configuration","By walking a signed chain of entity statements to a trust anchor it holds"],"answer":3,"why":"Trust is transitive through the anchor rather than pairwise, which is what makes onboarding a registration rather than N integrations.","whyWrong":["No secret is shared. The chain is verified with public keys alone.","DNS proves a host exists. It says nothing about policy or membership.","Anyone can publish one. Trust comes from the chain reaching your anchor.",""]},{"q":"An entity declares client_secret_basic but the anchor policy allows only private_key_jwt and mTLS. What happens?","options":["The anchor is updated to allow client_secret_basic","It is rejected, policies compose downward and can only narrow","It falls back to the default authentication method","It is accepted, entities declare their own metadata"],"answer":1,"why":"This is how an ecosystem enforces a security baseline on participants it does not operate, without relying on their honesty.","whyWrong":["Members do not amend the anchor.","","There is no widening fallback.","Self-declaration is exactly what policy constrains."]},{"q":"What is the greatest risk in a federation of this kind?","options":["Entity metadata growing large as the federation adds members","Slow DNS resolution when fetching each entity's statement","Trust anchor compromise, it takes the entire ecosystem with it","Long trust chains, since each extra hop weakens the signature"],"answer":2,"why":"Anchor keys belong offline with a rehearsed rotation. It is the trust-anchor lesson at maximum stakes.","whyWrong":["Bigger metadata costs bandwidth and cache space, nothing more.","Lookup latency slows discovery, it never changes who you trust.","","Each hop is verified on its own, so chain length costs time and nothing else."]},{"q":"When is bilateral registration still the better choice?","options":["For a handful of integrations that rarely change","For national ecosystems with many participants","For wallet verifiers meeting new issuers","Always, whatever the number of partners"],"answer":0,"why":"The crossover is somewhere in the tens of participants, or wherever participants change often.","whyWrong":["","At that scale you need federation, not pairwise setup.","A verifier must accept issuers it has never contacted.","Pairwise setup stops scaling once partners run into the dozens."]}],"ao8":[{"q":"What does CAE change about token handling?","options":["Tokens are validated by the issuer on every call","Tokens become opaque, so a resource server can no longer read claims without introspecting","The issuer pushes an event when something changes, so long-lived tokens can be rejected immediately","Tokens are encrypted, so only the intended resource server can read the claims they contain"],"answer":2,"why":"The token is unchanged and still cryptographically valid; the verifier now holds a fact that overrides it.","whyWrong":["That is introspection, which reintroduces the round trip.","The format is unchanged.","","Encryption is unrelated."]},{"q":"What is a Security Event Token?","options":["An access token with extra scopes","An encrypted session cookie shared between apps","A JWT whose payload is an event, not an identity","A refresh token used between providers"],"answer":2,"why":"It must be verified exactly like any other token, an unauthenticated revocation endpoint is a denial-of-service tool.","whyWrong":["It grants no access.","It is not a cookie and it carries no session.","","It is not a credential and grants nothing."]},{"q":"What is the real cost of participating in CAE?","options":["Larger tokens, since each one has to carry the claims needed to evaluate a continuous policy","The resource server becomes stateful, it must keep a revocation list and consult it","License fees, since participating requires a premium tier from the identity provider","Slower signatures, since every token must be re-signed whenever the evaluated context changes"],"answer":1,"why":"That is precisely the property self-contained tokens were adopted to avoid, so it is a considered trade rather than a free win.","whyWrong":["Token size is unchanged.","","Not a commercial matter.","Verification cost is unchanged."]},{"q":"Push delivery of an event fails and nobody notices. What is the danger?","options":["Duplicate events arriving each time the sender retries delivery","Events arriving out of order, so a stale state overwrites a newer one","Tokens start expiring early because the receiver falls behind","The system degrades silently into no revocation, which looks fine"],"answer":3,"why":"The worst failure mode is the one that looks healthy. Periodic reconciliation, or a short enough lifetime, is still required.","whyWrong":["The jti replay check already handles duplicates.","A real concern, and silent loss is the worse outcome.","Expiry is set at issue, and delivery failures do not touch it.",""]},{"q":"Does CAE make token revocation instant?","options":["Only for refresh tokens, since access tokens remain valid until their own expiry regardless","Yes, completely, since the event reaches every relying party before the next request is served","Yes, provided tokens are opaque, since the resource server must introspect each one anyway","No, it narrows the window from minutes to seconds, but a token already in flight is still honored"],"answer":3,"why":"No mechanism recalls a token already in flight. Layer it: short lifetimes as the floor, CAE where seconds matter, grant revocation to stop continued access.","whyWrong":["It applies to access tokens too.","Nothing is instant across a network.","Opaque tokens use introspection instead.",""]}],"iddid2":[{"q":"In the wallet model, who does the verifier contact?","options":["Nobody, the credential is verified from its signature, so the issuer never learns where it was used","The wallet vendor, which vouches that the holder's device has not been tampered with","A central registry, which records every presentation so that issuers can audit their credentials","The issuer, to confirm the credential is still valid before accepting what the holder presented"],"answer":0,"why":"That absence is the substantive difference from federation, where the IdP is online at the moment of use and therefore sees every login.","whyWrong":["","The vendor is not in the loop.","Registries help find issuers, not to verify each use.","Contacting the issuer would recreate the surveillance surface."]},{"q":"A bar needs to check you are over 18. What should it ask for?","options":["Your date of birth","An age_over_18 predicate claim","Your full credential","Your name and address"],"answer":1,"why":"Ask for the predicate, not the data. Most systems that store dates of birth only ever needed a boolean.","whyWrong":["More than the question requires.","","Maximal over-disclosure.","Entirely unnecessary."]},{"q":"What is the mDL, and why does it differ from OID4VP?","options":["A competing wallet application that implements the same protocol under a different brand","A credential format used only within the EU, which OID4VP supersedes elsewhere in the world","An older revision of OID4VP that remains in place until deployments finish migrating","An ISO standard for mobile driving licenses, using CBOR and designed to work offline over NFC or Bluetooth"],"answer":3,"why":"It came from standards bodies serving physical documents rather than from the web. Both are deployed, and convergence is partial.","whyWrong":["It is a standard, not an app.","Adoption is wider than one region.","They developed in parallel, not in sequence.",""]},{"q":"Why is credential revocation awkward in this model?","options":["Wallets cannot be updated once a credential is stored in them","Checking a status list can reintroduce a call back to the issuer","Credentials never expire, so there is no natural cutoff at all","Issuers refuse to publish revocation data for privacy reasons"],"answer":1,"why":"The privacy problem returns through the back door, which is why revocation remains one of the unsettled areas.","whyWrong":["Wallets update like any other app, and credentials can be reissued.","","They carry an expiry. Revocation is about ending validity early.","Issuers do publish status mechanisms. The question is how they get queried."]},{"q":"What does verifiable presentation NOT solve about age assurance?","options":["Handing over a full birthdate when only an age check is needed","Somebody still had to verify the underlying fact against a document","Proving to the verifier that the claim was not tampered with","Working when the verifier has no network connection at all"],"answer":1,"why":"The privacy question moves rather than disappearing: from what the site learns to who issued this and what they retained.","whyWrong":["Selective disclosure covers that. The presentation can assert over-18 alone.","","The signature chain handles that, and it holds up fine.","mDL is built for offline checks, reader to holder, with no call to the issuer."]}],"ig7":[{"q":"Roughly how do non-human identities compare in number to human ones?","options":["Far fewer, since one service account serves many people at once","It varies with no pattern, depending entirely on the industry","About equal, roughly one machine identity per employee","They outnumber humans substantially, commonly ten to one or more"],"answer":3,"why":"And almost every governance control in the domain was designed for the humans, which is why NHIs are more numerous, more privileged and less governed.","whyWrong":["The count runs the other way. Machine identities pile up fastest.","The ratio leans the same direction almost everywhere.","Most estates are nowhere near one to one.",""]},{"q":"Why does joiner-mover-leaver not work for service accounts?","options":["HR has no record of them, so the joiner form is never filled in","They cannot be disabled once an application depends on them","Reviews already cover them, so a separate lifecycle is redundant","Nothing signals that a service account is finished, so nothing ever removes it"],"answer":3,"why":"Estates accumulate them monotonically. An expiry that must be renewed converts \"forever\" into a deliberate decision.","whyWrong":["That is a symptom. A perfect HR record still would not say when the job ended.","They can be disabled. Nobody dares, which is a different problem.","Access reviews rarely reach service accounts, and rubber-stamping is the norm.",""]},{"q":"What is the single most common finding about non-human identities?","options":["No current owner, nobody knows what it does, so nobody will disable it","Names that ignore the convention agreed for service accounts","Missing tags, so the account never shows up in a cost report","Weak passwords that nobody has rotated since the account was made"],"answer":0,"why":"An identity without a named, current owner is ungovernable by definition.","whyWrong":["","Cosmetic. A badly named account with a known owner is still governable.","Also cosmetic, and tags do not tell you who to ask before switching it off.","Common enough, but downstream of ownership. No owner means nobody to rotate them."]},{"q":"What is the strongest control for a workload credential?","options":["Storing the secret encrypted at rest with an envelope key","A longer secret, well past the reach of any brute force attempt","Workload identity federation, no static credential exists at all","Quarterly rotation driven by a reminder on the team calendar"],"answer":2,"why":"There is then nothing to leak, rotate, or find in a repository.","whyWrong":["It has to be decrypted to be used, and that is where it leaks.","Length stops guessing. It does nothing about a secret that leaks.","","Rotation shortens the window and still leaves a secret to steal."]},{"q":"Why do reviewers rubber-stamp non-human access reviews?","options":["The tooling is slow, so reviewers batch-approve to get through the queue before it times out","They are lazy, and the review is one more task competing with work they consider more valuable","Reviews come round too frequently, so the same entries are approved repeatedly without fresh thought","Nobody recognizes svc-etl-prod-3, so without evidence of what it does they cannot make a real decision"],"answer":3,"why":"The fix is better evidence, last run, what it accessed, who owns it, not more reviews. Asking anyway trains people that reviews are theatre.","whyWrong":["Tooling speed is not the barrier.","They are given an impossible task.","Frequency is not the barrier.",""]}],"idf2b":[{"q":"A workforce JWT has grown past 4 KB. What goes wrong first, and how does it present?","options":["The signature stops verifying, because the payload no longer matches the header","The browser drops the cookie, with no error anywhere","The authorization server refuses to issue a token that large and returns an error","Nothing at all until the token passes the 8 KB header limit at the proxy"],"answer":1,"why":"The cookie ceiling is the first one you meet, and browsers enforce it by discarding the cookie rather than reporting anything. The user appears to be logged out at random and no log records a failure.","whyWrong":["Size does not change the signature; a truncating proxy can cause that later, but the cookie is dropped well before then.","","Issuers happily mint tokens far larger than any browser or proxy will carry. The limit is downstream, not at the issuer.","The 8 KB header limit is the second wall. The 4 KB cookie limit arrives first and is quieter."]},{"q":"An account is disabled at 09:00 and its access tokens live 60 minutes. When does access actually stop?","options":["At 10:00 at the latest, when the current token expires","Immediately, because disabling the account revokes every token it was issued","On the next request, since the resource server rechecks the directory each time","Never, because a self-contained token has no way to stop being valid"],"answer":0,"why":"Revocation lag is the token lifetime. A self-contained token is verified against its signature and its exp, not against the directory, so it keeps working until it expires.","whyWrong":["","Disabling the account changes the directory. It cannot reach into tokens already minted and in someone’s hands.","Consulting the directory on every request is the stateful design that tokens were adopted to avoid.","Tokens do stop being valid: at exp. The problem is the wait, not the absence of an end."]},{"q":"A user logs out and your app deletes the cookie holding their JWT. What has been revoked?","options":["The token, because the logout endpoint tells the issuer to revoke it","The token’s signature, which the issuer marks as invalid at logout","Every token issued in that session, because the audience refuses reused tokens","Nothing. Only the browser’s copy is gone"],"answer":3,"why":"Deleting the cookie removes one copy of the bytes. Any copy captured earlier is still signed, still unexpired and still accepted until exp.","whyWrong":["A local logout does not call anything. Even an RP-initiated logout at the issuer leaves already-issued access tokens working.","A signature is arithmetic over the token’s own bytes. There is no registry of signatures to mark.","A resource server has no memory of which tokens it has already seen, which is what makes replay possible in the first place.",""]},{"q":"A first-party web app on one domain, one backend you control, needs instant logout and hands a token to nobody. What should carry identity?","options":["A JWT in localStorage, so the app can scale horizontally later on","A JWT in a cookie, so that clearing the cookie logs the user out","An opaque session id in a cookie","A JWT in a header, plus a deny-list for the revoked ones"],"answer":2,"why":"Every condition that makes a token worth its costs is absent, and the requirement tokens are worst at, immediate logout, is present. Thirty-two opaque bytes stay small, reveal nothing and die the moment you delete the row.","whyWrong":["localStorage is readable by any script on the page, and horizontal scaling you have not measured is not a requirement.","Clearing the cookie removes the client’s copy, not the token. Any captured copy keeps working until exp.","","The deny-list is a session store. Having built one, you are paying for a lookup and carrying the token’s size as well."]}]};
